{"version":1,"claims":[{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"2020arXiv201201455O","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"7fd838dd5f6c6021","fields":{"author":"{Oriola}, David and {Marin-Riera}, Miquel and {Anlas}, Kerim and {Gritti}, Nicola and {Matsumiya}, Marina and {Aalderink}, Germaine and {Ebisuya}, Miki and {Sharpe}, James and {Trivedi}, Vikas","title":"{Arrested coalescence of multicellular aggregates}","journal":"arXiv e-prints","keywords":"Condensed Matter - Soft Condensed Matter, Quantitative Biology - Tissues and Organs","year":"2020","month":"dec","eid":"arXiv:2012.01455","pages":"arXiv:2012.01455","archiveprefix":"arXiv","eprint":"2012.01455","primaryclass":"cond-mat.soft","adsurl":"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2020arXiv201201455O","adsnote":"Provided by the SAO/NASA Astrophysics Data System"},"diffs":[{"field":"year","in_document":"2020","in_source":"2022","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Abaurrea2019","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"7e0b022ea3779d4e","fields":{"title":"Vesicles with internal active filaments: self-organized propulsion controls shape, motility, and dynamical response","author":"Abaurrea-Velasco, Clara and Auth, Thorsten and Gompper, Gerhard","journal":"New Journal of Physics","volume":"21","number":"12","pages":"123024","year":"2019","publisher":"IOP Publishing"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Amack2012a","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Successful embryogenesis requires proper sorting and compartmentalization of different cell types. Mechanical interactions between cells help govern these processes. In the past, physics-based theories have guided in vitro studies of cell sorting and tissue surface tension. Recent experiments have challenged this approach, indicating that adhesive molecules also act as signaling molecules that initiate local reorganization of actomyosin and demonstrating that cells at the boundary of a colony of initially identical cells become \"mechanically polarized.\" Extending physical models to account for mechanical polarization helps solve a long-standing paradox about magnitudes of tissue surface tensions and potentially explains discrepancies between recent in vivo and in vitro cell-sorting experiments. New experiments are needed to further explore the connection between mechanical polarization and tissue boundary formation in vivo.","author":"Amack, Jeffrey D. and Manning, M. Lisa","booktitle":"Science (80-. ).","doi":"10.1126/science.1223953","issn":"10959203","title":"{Knowing the boundaries: Extending the differential adhesion hypothesis in embryonic cell sorting}","year":"2012"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Athirasala2017","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"b8b9f199ce54ffb7","fields":{"title":"Nuclear mechanotransduction: sensing the force from within","author":"Athirasala, Avathamsa and Hirsch, Nivi and Buxboim, Amnon","journal":"Current opinion in cell biology","volume":"46","pages":"119--127","year":"2017","publisher":"Elsevier"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Barton2017","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"46caaa56145b375e","fields":{"title":"Active vertex model for cell-resolution description of epithelial tissue mechanics","author":"Barton, Daniel L. and Henkes, Silke and Weijer, Cornelis J and Sknepnek, Rastko","journal":"PLoS Computational Biology","volume":"13","number":"6","pages":"e1005569","year":"2017","publisher":"Public Library of Science San Francisco, CA USA"},"diffs":[{"field":"year","in_document":"2017","in_source":"2016","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Bayly2013","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"8a6ae6d9ff8e41b0","fields":{"title":"A cortical folding model incorporating stress-dependent growth explains gyral wavelengths and stress patterns in the developing brain","author":"Bayly, PV and Okamoto, RJ and Xu, G and Shi, Y and Taber, LA","journal":"Physical Biology","volume":"10","number":"1","pages":"016005","year":"2013","publisher":"IOP Publishing"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Beaune8055","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"author":"Beaune, Gr{\\'e}gory and Stirbat, Tomita Vasilica and Khalifat, Nada and Cochet-Escartin, Olivier and Garcia, Simon and Gurchenkov, Vasily Val{\\'e}r{\\\"\\i}{\\'e}vitch and Murrell, Michael P. and Dufour, Sylvie and Cuvelier, Damien and Brochard-Wyart, Fran{\\c c}oise","title":"How cells flow in the spreading of cellular aggregates","volume":"111","number":"22","pages":"8055--8060","year":"2014","doi":"10.1073/pnas.1323788111","publisher":"National Academy of Sciences","issn":"0027-8424","url":"https://www.pnas.org/content/111/22/8055","eprint":"https://www.pnas.org/content/111/22/8055.full.pdf","journal":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Belaghzal2021","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bf24426a4b7b840","fields":{"title":"Liquid chromatin Hi-C characterizes compartment-dependent chromatin interaction dynamics","author":"Belaghzal, Houda and Borrman, Tyler and Stephens, Andrew D and Lafontaine, Denis L and Venev, Sergey V and Weng, Zhiping and Marko, John F and Dekker, Job","journal":"Nature genetics","volume":"53","number":"3","pages":"367--378","year":"2021","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group US New York"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Berg2022","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"1718fbc8f07285d4","fields":{"title":"Transcription regulates bleb formation and stability independent of nuclear rigidity","author":"Berg, Isabel K and Currey, Marilena L and Gupta, Sarthak and Berrada, Yasmin and Nyugen Viet, Bao and Pho, Mai and Patteson, Alison E. and Schwarz, J. M.  and Banigan, Edward J and Stephens, Andrew D","journal":"bioRxiv","pages":"2022--11","year":"2022","publisher":"Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Berg2023","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"b8a637c81001054b","fields":{"title":"Transcription inhibition suppresses nuclear blebbing and rupture independently of nuclear rigidity","author":"Berg, Isabel K and Currey, Marilena L and Gupta, Sarthak and Berrada, Yasmin and Nguyen, Bao V and Pho, Mai and Patteson, Alison E and Schwarz, JM and Banigan, Edward J and Stephens, Andrew D","journal":"Journal of cell science","volume":"136","number":"20","pages":"jcs261547","year":"2023","publisher":"The Company of Biologists Ltd"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Bernabe2016","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"8d99f1993efc6b82","fields":{"title":"Novel role for the midbody in primary ciliogenesis by polarized epithelial cells","author":"Bernabe-Rubio, M. and et. al.","journal":"J. Cell Biol.","volume":"214","pages":"259-273","year":"2016"},"diffs":[{"field":"authors","in_document":"Bernabe-Rubio, M. and et. al.","in_source":"Miguel Bernabé-Rubio and Germán Andrés and Javier Casares-Arias and Jaime Fernández-Barrera and Laura Rangel and Natalia Reglero-Real and David C. Gershlick and José J. Fernández and Jaime Millán and Isabel Correas and David G. Miguez and Miguel A. Alonso","severity":"high"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Bi2015","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Cell migration is important in many biological processes, including embryonic development, cancer metastasis and wound healing. In these tissues, a cell's motion is often strongly constrained by its neighbours, leading to glassy dynamics. Although self-propelled particle models exhibit a density-driven glass transition, this does not explain liquid-to-solid transitions in confluent tissues, where there are no gaps between cells and therefore the density is constant. Here we demonstrate the existence of a new type of rigidity transition that occurs in the well-studied vertex model for confluent tissue monolayers at constant density. We find that the onset of rigidity is governed by a model parameter that encodes single-cell properties such as cell-cell adhesion and cortical tension, providing an explanation for liquid-to-solid transitions in confluent tissues and making testable predictions about how these transitions differ from those in particulate matter.","author":"Bi, Dapeng and Lopez, J. H. and Schwarz, J. M. and Manning, M. Lisa","doi":"10.1038/nphys3471","file":":C\\:/Users/Tao/AppData/Local/Mendeley Ltd./Mendeley Desktop/Downloaded/Bi et al. - 2015 - A density-independent rigidity transition in biological tissues.pdf:pdf","issn":"17452481","journal":"Nature Physics","mendeley-groups":"SJTU/JM Schwarz/BRAIN_ORGANOIDS,SJTU/JM Schwarz,SJTU/JM Schwarz/references","number":"12","pages":"1074--1079","title":"{A density-independent rigidity transition in biological tissues}","volume":"11","year":"2015"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Bi2016b","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Cell motion inside dense tissues governs many biological processes, including embryonic development and cancer metastasis, and recent experiments suggest that these tissues exhibit collective glassy behavior. To make quantitative predictions about glass transitions in tissues, we study a self-propelled Voronoi model that simultaneously captures polarized cell motility and multibody cell-cell interactions in a confluent tissue, where there are no gaps between cells. We demonstrate that the model exhibits a jamming transition from a solidlike state to a fluidlike state that is controlled by three parameters: the single-cell motile speed, the persistence time of single-cell tracks, and a target shape index that characterizes the competition between cell-cell adhesion and cortical tension. In contrast to traditional particulate glasses, we are able to identify an experimentally accessible structural order parameter that specifies the entire jamming surface as a function of model parameters. We demonstrate that a continuum soft glassy rheology model precisely captures this transition in the limit of small persistence times and explain how it fails in the limit of large persistence times. These results provide a framework for understanding the collective solid-to-liquid transitions that have been observed in embryonic development and cancer progression, which may be associated with epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition in these tissues.","archiveprefix":"arXiv","arxivid":"1509.06578","author":"Bi, Dapeng and Yang, Xingbo and Marchetti, M. Cristina and Manning, M. Lisa","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevX.6.021011","eprint":"1509.06578","issn":"21603308","journal":"Phys. Rev. X","title":"{Motility-driven glass and jamming transitions in biological tissues}","year":"2016"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Bi2021","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Cell neighbor exchanges are integral to tissue rearrangements in biology, including development and repair. Often, these processes occur via topological T1 transitions analogous to those observed in foams, grains, and colloids. However, in contrast to those in nonliving materials, the T1 transitions in biological tissues are rate limited and cannot occur instantaneously due to the finite time required to remodel complex structures at cell-cell junctions. Here, we study how this rate-limiting process affects the mechanics and collective behavior of cells in a tissue by introducing this important biological constraint in a theoretical vertex-based model as an intrinsic single-cell property. We report that, in the absence of this time constraint, the tissue undergoes a glass transition with lowering of cell motility characterized by a sharp increase in the intermittency of cell-cell rearrangements. Remarkably, this glass transition disappears, as T1 transitions are temporally limited. As a unique consequence of limited rearrangements, we also find that the tissue develops spatially correlated populations of fast and slow cells, in which the fast cells organize into streamlike patterns and maintain optimally stable cell-cell contacts. The predictions of this work are compared with existing in vivo experiments in Drosophila pupal development.","archiveprefix":"arXiv","arxivid":"2003.01042","author":"Das, Amit and Sastry, Srikanth and Bi, Dapeng","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevX.11.041037","eprint":"2003.01042","file":":C\\:/Users/Tao/Dropbox/SJTU/VertexModel/references/PhysRevX.11.041037.pdf:pdf","issn":"21603308","journal":"Physical Review X","keywords":"biological physics,doi:10.1103/PhysRevX.11.041037 url:https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevX.11.041037,soft matter","mendeley-groups":"SJTU/JM Schwarz/references","number":"4","pages":"41037","publisher":"American Physical Society","title":"{Controlled Neighbor Exchanges Drive Glassy Behavior, Intermittency, and Cell Streaming in Epithelial Tissues}","url":"https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevX.11.041037","volume":"11","year":"2021"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Birkhoff2021","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"74fc27bdbd12c9c5","fields":{"title":"ZEB2, the Mowat-Wilson syndrome transcription factor: Confirmations, novel functions, and continuing surprises","author":"Birkhoff, Judith C and Huylebroeck, Danny and Conidi, Andrea","journal":"Genes","volume":"12","number":"7","pages":"1037","year":"2021","publisher":"MDPI"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Bonev2016","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"8a5998c300b0621f","fields":{"title":"Organization and function of the 3D genome","author":"Bonev, Boyan and Cavalli, Giacomo","journal":"Nature Reviews Genetics","volume":"17","number":"11","pages":"661--678","year":"2016","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group UK London"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Borzou2021","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0a42f35a45a99632","fields":{"author":"{Borzou}, Ahmad and {Patteson}, Alison E. and {Schwarz}, J.~M.","title":"{A Data-Driven Statistical Description for the Hydrodynamics of Active Matter}","journal":"arXiv e-prints","keywords":"Condensed Matter - Soft Condensed Matter, Condensed Matter - Statistical Mechanics","year":"2021","month":"mar","eid":"arXiv:2103.03461","pages":"arXiv:2103.03461","archiveprefix":"arXiv","eprint":"2103.03461","primaryclass":"cond-mat.soft","adsurl":"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2021arXiv210303461B","adsnote":"Provided by the SAO/NASA Astrophysics Data System"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Borzou2022","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"5c05f1bce5c352b8","fields":{"title":"Large-scale cortex-core structure formation in brain organoids","author":"Borzou, Ahmad and Schwarz, JM","journal":"Frontiers in Physics","volume":"10","pages":"837600","year":"2022","publisher":"Frontiers"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Brandt2005","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"b85bed35d83c1f81","fields":{"title":"Three-dimensional average-shape atlas of the honeybee brain and its applications","journal":"J. Comp. Neurol.","volume":"492","pages":"1-19","year":"2005","author":"Brandt, R. and Rohlfing, R. and Rybak, J. and Krofczik, S. and Maye, A. and Westerhoff, M. and Hege, H.-C. and Menzel, R."},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Brickman2017","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"ecf98fcc00977e16","fields":{"author":"Brickman, J. M. and Serup, P.","title":"Properties of embryoid bodies","publisher":"","year":"2017","journal":"Rev. Dev. Biol.","volume":"6","number":"","pages":""},"diffs":[{"field":"year","in_document":"2017","in_source":"2016","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Brodland2002a","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"A comprehensive theory, herein named the Differential Interfacial Tension Hypothesis (DITH), for the self-rearrangement of embryonic cells and tissues is presented, These rearrangements include sorting, mixing and formation of checkerboard patterns in heterotypic aggregates of embryonic cells, and total or partial engulfment, separation and dissociation of tissues. This broadly-based theory accounts for the action of all currently known cytoskeletal components and cell adhesion mechanisms. The theory is used to derive conditions for the cell and tissue rearrangements named above, Finite element-based computer simulations involving two or more cell types confirm these conditions.","author":"Brodland, G. Wayne","doi":"10.1115/1.1449491","issn":"01480731","journal":"J. Biomech. Eng.","keywords":"Cell Mechanics,Cell Mixing,Cell Sorting,Checkerboard Patterns,Computer Simulations,Differential Adhesion Hypothesis (DAH),Differential Interfacial Tension Hypothesis (DITH),Finite Element Method (FEM),Tissue Engulfment","title":"{The Differential Interfacial Tension Hypothesis (DITH): A comprehensive theory for the self-rearrangement of embryonic cells and tissues}","year":"2002"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Browet2016","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"The progress in imaging techniques have allowed the study of various aspect of cellular mechanisms. To isolate individual cells in live imaging data, we introduce an elegant image segmentation framework that effectively extracts cell boundaries, even in the presence of poor edge details. Our approach works in two stages. First, we estimate pixel interior/border/exterior class probabilities using random ferns. Then, we use an energy minimization framework to compute boundaries whose localization is compliant with the pixel class probabilities. We validate our approach on a manually annotated dataset.","archiveprefix":"arXiv","arxivid":"1602.05439","author":"Browet, A. and {De Vleeschouwer}, C. and Jacques, L. and Mathiah, N. and Saykali, B. and Migeotte, I.","booktitle":"Proc. - Int. Conf. Image Process. ICIP","doi":"10.1109/ICIP.2016.7533140","eprint":"1602.05439","isbn":"9781467399616","issn":"15224880","keywords":"Cell segmentation,Fluorescent microscopy,Graph-cuts,Random ferns","title":"{Cell segmentation with random ferns and graph-cuts}","year":"2016"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Budday2015","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"1a7737f1723acc3c","fields":{"title":"Physical biology of human brain development","author":"Budday, Silvia and Steinmann III, Paul and Kuhl, Ellen","journal":"Frontiers in Cellular Neuroscience","volume":"9","pages":"257","year":"2015","publisher":"Frontiers"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Calzolari2014","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Segregating cells into compartments during embryonic development is essential for growth and pattern formation. Physical mechanisms shaping compartment boundaries were recently explored in Drosophila, where actomyosin-based barriers were revealed to be important for keeping cells apart. In vertebrates, interhombomeric boundaries are straight interfaces, which often serve as signaling centers that pattern the surrounding tissue. Here, we demonstrate that in the hindbrain of zebrafish embryos cell sorting sharpens the molecular boundaries and, once borders are straight, actomyosin barriers are key to keeping rhombomeric cells segregated. Actomyosin cytoskeletal components are enriched at interhombomeric boundaries, forming cable-like structures in the apical side of the neuroepithelial cells by the time morphological boundaries are visible. When myosin II function is inhibited, cable structures do not form, leading to rhombomeric cell mixing. Downregulation of EphA4a compromises actomyosin cables and cells with different rhombomeric identity intermingle, and the phenotype is rescued enhancing myosin II activity. Moreover, enrichment of actomyosin structures is obtained when EphA4 is ectopically expressed in even-numbered rhombomeres. These findings suggest that mechanical barriers act downstream of EphA/ephrin signaling to segregate cells from different rhombomeres. Synopsis Actomyosin cables form mechanical barriers in the hindbrain of zebrafish embryos that keep rhombomeric cells segregated. Segregating cells into compartments during embryonic development is essential for growth and pattern formation, ensuring that their fates and/or positional information remain segregated as they proliferate and move. The refinement of gene expression borders in the hindbrain is achieved by cell sorting. There are actomyosin cables in the interhombomeric boundaries: F-actin and myosin II components are enriched in the apical side of the neuroepithelial cells at the boundaries. Actomyosin barriers sort cells at rhombomeric boundaries: Inhibition of myosin II function results in dismantling of cable structures, leading to rhombomeric cell mixing. Conversely, ectopic activation of RhoA pathway leads to the enrichment of actomyosin structures. EphA/ephrin signaling regulates the formation of actomyosin cables through RhoA cascade, to segregate cells from different rhombomeres. {\\textcopyright} 2014 The Authors.","author":"Calzolari, Simone and Terriente, Javier and Pujades, Cristina","doi":"10.1002/embj.201386003","issn":"14602075","journal":"EMBO J.","keywords":"actomyosin cable,cell sorting,compartments,rhombomeric boundaries,segmentation","title":"{Cell segregation in the vertebrate hindbrain relies on actomyosin cables located at the interhombomeric boundaries}","year":"2014"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"CanongiaLopes2002","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Several binary systems composed of one-centre Lennard-Jones particles with identical size and interaction parameters, $\\sigma$11, $\\sigma$22, $\\epsilon$11, $\\epsilon$22 and $\\epsilon$12, but different cross diameter parameters $\\sigma$12, were simulated using an extended version of the Gibbs ensemble Monte Carlo method, in an attempt to predict the fluid phase behaviour in such systems. No liquid-liquid phase separation was found in the mixtures but all cases studied exhibit a structured liquid phase in equilibrium with its vapour. The domain shapes and patterns observed both in three- and two-dimensional simulations (stripe and bubble phases) were interpreted within the framework of the phenomenology of modulated phases, also known as microphase separated fluids. The appearance of stripe patterns occurs when $\\sigma$12 is larger than $\\sigma$11, while the bubble phases are characteristic of systems with $\\sigma$12 smaller than $\\sigma$11.","author":"{Canongia Lopes}, Jos{\\'{e}} N.","booktitle":"Phys. Chem. Chem. Phys.","doi":"10.1039/b108845a","issn":"14639076","title":"{Microphase separation in mixtures of Lennard-Jones particles}","year":"2002"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Canty2017a","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"The establishment of sharp boundaries is essential for segregation of embryonic tissues during development, but the underlying mechanism of cell sorting has remained unclear. Opposing hypotheses have been proposed, either based on global tissue adhesive or contractile properties or on local signalling through cell contact cues. Here we use ectoderm-mesoderm separation in Xenopus to directly evaluate the role of these various parameters. We find that ephrin-Eph-based repulsion is very effective at inducing and maintaining separation, whereas differences in adhesion or contractility have surprisingly little impact. Computer simulations support and generalise our experimental results, showing that a high heterotypic interfacial tension between tissues is key to their segregation. We propose a unifying model, in which conditions of sorting previously considered as driven by differential adhesion/tension should be viewed as suboptimal cases of heterotypic interfacial tension.The mechanisms that cause different cells to segregate into distinct tissues are unclear. Here the authors show in Xenopus that formation of a boundary between two tissues is driven by local tension along the interface rather than by global differences in adhesion or cortical contractility.","author":"Canty, Laura and Zarour, Eleyine and Kashkooli, Leily and Fran{\\c{c}}ois, Paul and Fagotto, Fran{\\c{c}}ois","doi":"10.1038/s41467-017-00146-x","volume":"8","pages":"157","journal":"Nat. Comm.","title":"{Sorting at embryonic boundaries requires high heterotypic interfacial tension}","year":"2017"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Cavanaugh2020","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"99027f940bc76cd2","fields":{"title":"RhoA mediates epithelial cell shape changes via mechanosensitive endocytosis","author":"Cavanaugh, Kate E. and Staddon, Michael F. and Munro, Edwin and Banerjee, Shiladitya and Gardel, Margaret L.","journal":"Developmental Cell","volume":"52","number":"2","pages":"152--166","year":"2020","publisher":"Elsevier"},"diffs":[{"field":"year","in_document":"2020","in_source":"2019","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Chenn2002","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0e64ca17c8c8e361","fields":{"title":"Regulation of cerebral cortical size by control of cell cycle exit in neural precursors","author":"Chenn, A. and Walsh, C. A.","journal":"Science","volume":"297","pages":"365-369","year":"2002"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Chenn2003","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"b9c077e55c3df895","fields":{"title":"Increased neuronal production, enlarged forebrains and cytoarchitectural distortions in beta-catenin overexpressing transgenic mice","author":"Chenn, A. and Walsh, C. A.","journal":"Cereb. Cortex","volume":"13","pages":"599-606","year":"2003"},"diffs":[{"field":"authors","in_document":"Chenn, A. and Walsh, C. A.","in_source":"A. Chenn","severity":"high"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Chiou2012","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Many morphogenetic processes involve mechanical rearrangements of epithelial tissues that are driven by precisely regulated cytoskeletal forces and cell adhesion. The mechanical state of the cell and intercellular adhesion are not only the targets of regulation, but are themselves the likely signals that coordinate developmental process. Yet, because it is difficult to directly measure mechanical stress in vivo on sub-cellular scale, little is understood about the role of mechanics in development. Here we present an alternative approach which takes advantage of the recent progress in live imaging of morphogenetic processes and uses computational analysis of high resolution images of epithelial tissues to infer relative magnitude of forces acting within and between cells. We model intracellular stress in terms of bulk pressure and interfacial tension, allowing these parameters to vary from cell to cell and from interface to interface. Assuming that epithelial cell layers are close to mechanical equilibrium, we use the observed geometry of the two dimensional cell array to infer interfacial tensions and intracellular pressures. Here we present the mathematical formulation of the proposed Mechanical Inverse method and apply it to the analysis of epithelial cell layers observed at the onset of ventral furrow formation in the Drosophila embryo and in the process of hair-cell determination in the avian cochlea. The analysis reveals mechanical anisotropy in the former process and mechanical heterogeneity, correlated with cell differentiation, in the latter process. The proposed method opens a way for quantitative and detailed experimental tests of models of cell and tissue mechanics.","archiveprefix":"arXiv","arxivid":"arXiv:1112.5905v1","author":"Chiou, Kevin K. and Hufnagel, Lars and Shraiman, Boris I.","doi":"10.1371/journal.pcbi.1002512","eprint":"arXiv:1112.5905v1","isbn":"1553-7358 (Electronic)$\\backslash$n1553-734X (Linking)","issn":"1553734X","journal":"PLoS Comput. Biol.","pmid":"22615550","title":"{Mechanical stress inference for two dimensional cell arrays}","year":"2012"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Chremos2014","verdict":"mismatch","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"In this contribution, we develop a coarse-graining methodology for mapping specific block copolymer systems to bead-spring particle-based models. We map the constituent Kuhn segments to Lennard-Jones particles, and establish a semi-empirical correlation between the experimentally determined Flory-Huggins parameter $\\chi$ and the interaction of the model potential. For these purposes, we have performed an extensive set of isobaric-isothermal Monte Carlo simulations of binary mixtures of Lennard-Jones particles with the same size but with asymmetric energetic parameters. The phase behavior of these monomeric mixtures is then extended to chains with finite sizes through theoretical considerations. Such a top-down coarse-graining approach is important from a computational point of view, since many characteristic features of block copolymer systems are on time and length scales which are still inaccessible through fully atomistic simulations. We demonstrate the applicability of our method for generating parameters by reproducing the morphology diagram of a specific diblock copolymer, namely, poly(styrene-b-methyl methacrylate), which has been extensively studied in experiments. {\\textcopyright} 2014 AIP Publishing LLC.","author":"Chremos, Alexandros and Nikoubashman, Arash and Panagiotopoulos, Athanassios Z.","doi":"10.1063/1.4863331","issn":"00219606","journal":"J. Chem. Phys.","title":"{Flory-Huggins parameter $\\chi$, from binary mixtures of Lennard-Jones particles to block copolymer melts}","year":"2014"},"diffs":[{"field":"title","in_document":"{Flory-Huggins parameter $\\chi$, from binary mixtures of Lennard-Jones particles to block copolymer melts}","in_source":"Flory-Huggins parameter χ, from binary mixtures of Lennard-Jones particles to block copolymer melts","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Chu2013","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"64f31f929ea3dc28","fields":{"title":"Lulu regulates Shroom-induced apical constriction during neural tube closure","author":"Chu, Chih-Wen and Gerstenzang, Emma and Ossipova, Olga and Sokol, Sergei Y","journal":"PLoS One","volume":"8","number":"11","pages":"e81854","year":"2013","publisher":"Public Library of Science San Francisco, USA"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Clevers2016","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"734c7b56a34dd5a5","fields":{"title":"Modeling development and disease with organoids","author":"Clevers, Hans","journal":"Cell","volume":"165","number":"7","pages":"1586--1597","year":"2016","publisher":"Elsevier"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Cochet-Escartin2017a","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Cell sorting, whereby a heterogeneous cell mixture organizes into distinct tissues, is a fundamental patterning process in development. Hydra is a powerful model system for carrying out studies of cell sorting in three dimensions, because of its unique ability to regenerate after complete dissociation into individual cells. The physicists Alfred Gierer and Hans Meinhardt recognized Hydra's self-organizing properties more than 40 years ago. However, what drives cell sorting during regeneration of Hydra from cell aggregates is still debated. Differential motility and differential adhesion have been proposed as driving mechanisms, but the available experimental data are insufficient to distinguish between these two. Here, we answer this longstanding question by using transgenic Hydra expressing fluorescent proteins and a multiscale experimental and numerical approach. By quantifying the kinematics of single cell and whole aggregate behaviors, we show that no differences in cell motility exist among cell types and that sorting dynamics follow a power law with an exponent of ∼0.5. Additionally, we measure the physical properties of separated tissues and quantify their viscosities and surface tensions. Based on our experimental results and numerical simulations, we conclude that tissue interfacial tensions are sufficient to explain cell sorting in aggregates of Hydra cells. Furthermore, we demonstrate that the aggregate's geometry during sorting is key to understanding the sorting dynamics and explains the exponent of the power law behavior. Our results answer the long standing question of the physical mechanisms driving cell sorting in Hydra cell aggregates. In addition, they demonstrate how powerful this organism is for biophysical studies of self-organization and pattern formation.","author":"Cochet-Escartin, Olivier and Locke, Tiffany T. and Shi, Winnie H. and Steele, Robert E. and Collins, Eva Maria S.","doi":"10.1016/j.bpj.2017.10.045","issn":"15420086","journal":"Biophys. J.","title":"{Physical Mechanisms Driving Cell Sorting in Hydra}","year":"2017"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Comelles2021","verdict":"unverified","source":"none","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"","fields":{"title":"Epithelial colonies in vitro elongate through collective effects","author":"Comelles, Jordi and Soumya, S. S. and Lu, Linjie and Le Maout, Emilie and Anvitha, Sudakar and Salbreux, Guillaume and J{\\\"u}licher, Frank and Inamdar, Mandar M. and Riveline, Daniel","journal":"Elife","volume":"10","pages":"e57730","year":"2021","publisher":"eLife Sciences Publications Limited"},"diffs":[],"failure_note":"not found at the registries — books published before DOIs often are not registered; the entry may still be correct"},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Dahl2006","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"7cbade3b0a67a8db","fields":{"title":"Distinct structural and mechanical properties of the nuclear lamina in Hutchinson--Gilford progeria syndrome","author":"Dahl, Kris Noel and Scaffidi, Paola and Islam, Mohammad F and Yodh, Arjun G and Wilson, Katherine L and Misteli, Tom","journal":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","volume":"103","number":"27","pages":"10271--10276","year":"2006","publisher":"National Acad Sciences"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Dahmann2011","verdict":"partial","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"The formation and maintenance of boundaries between neighbouring groups of embryonic cells is vital for development because groups of cells with distinct functions must often be kept physically separated. Furthermore, because cells at the boundary often take on important signalling functions by acting as organizing centres, boundary shape and integrity can also control the outcome of many downstream patterning events. Recent experimental findings and theoretical descriptions have shed new light on classic questions about boundaries. In particular, in the past couple of years the role of forces acting in epithelial tissues to maintain boundaries has emerged as a new principle in understanding how early pattern is made into permanent anatomy. {\\textcopyright} 2011 Macmillan Publishers Limited. All rights reserved.","author":"Dahmann, Christian and Oates, Andrew C. and Brand, Michael","booktitle":"Nat. Rev. Genet.","doi":"10.1038/nrg2902","issn":"14710056","title":"{Boundary formation and maintenance in tissue development}","year":"2011"},"diffs":[{"field":"year","in_document":"2011","in_source":"2010","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Davidson2014","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"ae05d53d5a5c3f67","fields":{"title":"Nuclear deformability constitutes a rate-limiting step during cell migration in 3-D environments","author":"Davidson, Patricia M and Denais, Celine and Bakshi, Maya C and Lammerding, Jan","journal":"Cellular and molecular bioengineering","volume":"7","pages":"293--306","year":"2014","publisher":"Springer"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Dekker2017","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"bbe53b1a6d91eb4f","fields":{"title":"The 4D nucleome project","author":"Dekker, Job and Belmont, Andrew S and Guttman, Mitchell and Leshyk, Victor O and Lis, John T and Lomvardas, Stavros and Mirny, Leonid A and O’shea, Clodagh C and Park, Peter J and Ren, Bing and others","journal":"Nature","volume":"549","number":"7671","pages":"219--226","year":"2017","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group UK London"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Deng2022","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0b14c70414b816dc","fields":{"author":"Yanxiang Deng  and Marek Bartosovic  and Petra Kukanja  and Di Zhang  and Yang Liu  and Graham Su  and Archibald Enninful  and Zhiliang Bai  and Gonçalo Castelo-Branco  and Rong Fan","title":"Spatial-CUT\\&amp;Tag: Spatially resolved chromatin modification profiling at the cellular level","journal":"Science","volume":"375","number":"6581","pages":"681-686","year":"2022"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"DevNervousSystemBook","verdict":"unverified","source":"none","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"","fields":{"title":"Development of the Nervous System","author":"Sanes, Dan H. and Reh, Thomas A. and Harris, William A.","year":"2012","publisher":"Academic Press"},"diffs":[],"failure_note":"not found at the registries — books published before DOIs often are not registered; the entry may still be correct"},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Dixon12","verdict":"unverified","source":"none","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"","fields":{"title":"Topological domains in mammalian genomes identified by analysis of chromatin interactions","author":"Wang, S. and Su, J. H. and Beliveau, B. J. and Bintu, B. and Moffitt, J. R. and Wu, C. and Zhuang, X.","journal":"Nature","volume":"485","number":"7398","pages":"376-380","year":"2012"},"diffs":[],"failure_note":"not found at the registries — books published before DOIs often are not registered; the entry may still be correct"},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Duclut2021","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"2b1e604af688fcb7","fields":{"title":"Nonlinear rheology of cellular networks","author":"Duclut, Charlie and Paijmans, Joris and Inamdar, Mandar M. and Modes, Carl D. and J{\\\"u}licher, Frank","journal":"Cells \\& Development","pages":"203746","year":"2021","publisher":"Elsevier"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Ebersberger2002","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"a27fb9beac81d8bb","fields":{"title":"Genomewide comparison of DNA sequences between humans and chimpanzees","author":"Ebersberger, Ingo and Metzler, Dirk and Schwarz, Carsten and P{\\\"a}{\\\"a}bo, Svante","journal":"The American Journal of Human Genetics","volume":"70","number":"6","pages":"1490--1497","year":"2002","publisher":"Elsevier"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Engl2014","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Mechanical stress is increasingly being shown to be a potent modulator of cell-cell junctional morphologies in developmental and homeostatic processes. Intercellular force sensing is thus expected to be an important regulator of cell signalling and tissue integrity. In particular, the interplay between myosin contractility, actin dynamics and E-cadherin recruitment largely remains to be uncovered. We devised a suspended cell doublet assay to quantitatively assess the correlation between myosin II activity and local E-cadherin recruitment. The single junction of the doublet exhibited a stereotypical morphology, with E-cadherin accumulating into clusters of varied concentrations at the rim of the circular contact. This local recruitment into clusters derived from the sequestration of E-cadherin through a myosin-II-driven modulation of actin turnover. We exemplify how the regulation of actin dynamics provides a mechanism for the mechanosensitive response of cell contacts. {\\textcopyright} 2014 Macmillan Publishers Limited. All rights reserved.","author":"Engl, W. and Arasi, B. and Yap, L. L. and Thiery, J. P. and Viasnoff, V.","doi":"10.1038/ncb2973","issn":"14764679","journal":"Nat. Cell Biol.","title":"{Actin dynamics modulate mechanosensitive immobilization of E-cadherin at adherens junctions}","year":"2014"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Engstrom2018","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"618333fcebcc6bb9","fields":{"title":"Buckling without bending: a new paradigm in morphogenesis","author":"Engstrom, TA and Zhang, Teng and Lawton, AK and Joyner, AL and Schwarz, Jennifer M","journal":"Physical Review X","volume":"8","number":"4","pages":"041053","year":"2018","publisher":"APS"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Eres2019","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"f64197c4ffb3d263","fields":{"title":"Reorganization of 3D genome structure may contribute to gene regulatory evolution in primates","author":"Eres, Ittai E and Luo, Kaixuan and Hsiao, Chiaowen Joyce and Blake, Lauren E and Gilad, Yoav","journal":"PLoS Genetics","volume":"15","number":"7","pages":"e1008278","year":"2019","publisher":"Public Library of Science San Francisco, CA USA"},"diffs":[{"field":"year","in_document":"2019","in_source":"2018","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Farhadifar2007a","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Background: Epithelial junctional networks assume packing geometries characterized by different cell shapes, neighbor number distributions and areas. The development of specific packing geometries is tightly controlled; in the Drosophila wing epithelium, cells convert from an irregular to a hexagonal array shortly before hair formation. Packing geometry is determined by developmental mechanisms that likely control the biophysical properties of cells and their interactions. Results: To understand how physical cellular properties and proliferation determine cell-packing geometries, we use a vertex model for the epithelial junctional network in which cell packing geometries correspond to stable and stationary network configurations. The model takes into account cell elasticity and junctional forces arising from cortical contractility and adhesion. By numerically simulating proliferation, we generate different network morphologies that depend on physical parameters. These networks differ in polygon class distribution, cell area variation, and the rate of T1 and T2 transitions during growth. Comparing theoretical results to observed cell morphologies reveals regions of parameter space where calculated network morphologies match observed ones. We independently estimate parameter values by quantifying network deformations caused by laser ablating individual cell boundaries. Conclusions: The vertex model accounts qualitatively and quantitatively for the observed packing geometry in the wing disc and its response to perturbation by laser ablation. Epithelial packing geometry is a consequence of both physical cellular properties and the disordering influence of proliferation. The occurrence of T2 transitions during network growth suggests that elimination of cells from the proliferating disc epithelium may be the result of junctional force balances. ?? 2007 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.","author":"Farhadifar, Reza and R{\\\"{o}}per, Jens Christian and Aigouy, Benoit and Eaton, Suzanne and J{\\\"{u}}licher, Frank","doi":"10.1016/j.cub.2007.11.049","file":":Users/prsahu/Library/Application Support/Mendeley Desktop/Downloaded/Farhadifar et al. - 2007 - The Influence of Cell Mechanics, Cell-Cell Interactions, and Proliferation on Epithelial Packing.pdf:pdf","isbn":"0960-9822","issn":"09609822","journal":"Curr. Biol.","keywords":"DEVBIO","number":"24","pages":"2095--2104","pmid":"18082406","title":"{The Influence of Cell Mechanics, Cell-Cell Interactions, and Proliferation on Epithelial Packing}","volume":"17","year":"2007"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Fernandez-de-Manuel2017","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Embryonic stem cells (ESCs) can be established as permanent cell lines, and their potential to differentiate into adult tissues has led to widespread use for studying the mechanisms and dynamics of stem cell differentiation and exploring strategies for tissue repair. Imaging live ESCs during development is now feasible due to advances in optical imaging and engineering of genetically encoded fluorescent reporters; however, a major limitation is the low spatio-temporal resolution of long-term 3-D imaging required for generational and neighboring reconstruc­tions. Here, we present the ESC-Track (ESC-T) workflow, which includes an automated cell and nuclear segmen­tation and tracking tool for 4-D (3-D + time) confocal image data sets as well as a manual editing tool for visual inspection and error correction. ESC-T automatically identifies cell divisions and membrane contacts for lineage tree and neighborhood reconstruction and computes quantitative features from individual cell entities, enabling analysis of fluorescence signal dynamics and tracking of cell morphology and motion. We use ESC-T to examine Myc intensity fluctuations in the context of mouse ESC (mESC) lineage and neighborhood relationships. ESC-T is a powerful tool for evaluation of the genealogical and microenvironmental cues that maintain ESC fitness.","author":"Fern{\\'{a}}ndez-de-Man{\\'{u}}el, Laura and D{\\'{i}}az-D{\\'{i}}az, Covadonga and Jim{\\'{e}}nez-Carretero, Daniel and Torres, Miguel and Montoya, Mar{\\'{i}}a C.","doi":"10.2144/000114545","issn":"19409818","journal":"Biotechniques","keywords":"ESC,Image processing,Lineage,Segmentation,Tracking","title":"{ESC-track: A computer workflow for 4-D segmentation, tracking, lineage tracing and dynamic context analysis of ESCs}","year":"2017"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Finegan2019","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"ba0276a95e05fe72","fields":{"title":"The tricellular vertex-specific adhesion molecule Sidekick facilitates polarised cell intercalation during Drosophila axis extension","author":"Finegan, Tara M. and Hervieux, Nathan and Nestor-Bergmann, Alexander and Fletcher, Alexander G. and Blanchard, Guy B. and Sanson, B{\\'e}n{\\'e}dicte","journal":"PLoS Biology","volume":"17","number":"12","pages":"e3000522","year":"2019","publisher":"Public Library of Science San Francisco, CA USA"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Fletcher2013","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"aa3b48f286b728de","fields":{"title":"Implementing vertex dynamics models of cell populations in biology within a consistent computational framework","author":"Fletcher, Alexander G. and Osborne, James M. and Maini, Philip K. and Gavaghan, David J.","journal":"Progress in Biophysics and Molecular Biology","volume":"113","number":"2","pages":"299--326","year":"2013","publisher":"Elsevier"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Fletcher2014","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"57e63e8b3fa96af2","fields":{"title":"Vertex models of epithelial morphogenesis","author":"Fletcher, Alexander G. and Osterfield, Miriam and Baker, Ruth E. and Shvartsman, Stanislav Y.","journal":"Biophysical Journal","volume":"106","number":"11","pages":"2291--2304","year":"2014","publisher":"Elsevier"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Foty2005","verdict":"partial","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"The differential adhesion hypothesis (DAH), advanced in the 1960s, proposed that the liquid-like tissue-spreading and cell segregation phenomena of development arise from tissue surface tensions that in turn arise from differences in intercellular adhesiveness. Our earlier measurements of liquid-like cell aggregate surface tensions have shown that, without exception, a cell aggregate of lower surface tension tends to envelop one of higher surface tension to which it adheres. We here measure the surface tensions of L cell aggregates transfected to express N-, P- or E-cadherin in varied, measured amounts. We report that in these aggregates, in which cadherins are essentially the only cell-cell adhesion molecules, the aggregate surface tensions are a direct, linear function of cadherin expression level. Taken together with our earlier results, the conclusion follows that the liquid-like morphogenetic cell and tissue rearrangements of cell sorting, tissue spreading and segregation represent self-assembly processes guided by the diminution of adhesive-free energy as cells tend to maximize their mutual binding. This conclusion relates to the physics governing these morphogenetic phenomena and applies independently of issues such as the specificities of intercellular adhesives. {\\textcopyright} 2004 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.","author":"Foty, Ramsey A. and Steinberg, Malcolm S.","doi":"10.1016/j.ydbio.2004.11.012","issn":"00121606","journal":"Dev. Biol.","keywords":"Cadherins,Cell binding energy,Cell sorting,Cell-cell adhesion,Differential adhesion,Sorting-out,Surface tension,Tissue affinities,Tissue spreading","title":"{The differential adhesion hypothesis: A direct evaluation}","year":"2005"},"diffs":[{"field":"year","in_document":"2005","in_source":"2004","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Fouani2020","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"776f7f9a49a4e912","fields":{"title":"During mitosis ZEB1 “switches” from being a chromatin-bound epithelial gene repressor, to become a microtubule-associated protein","author":"Fouani, L and Huang, MLH and Cole, L and Jansson, PJ and Kovacevic, Z and Richardson, DR","journal":"Biochimica et Biophysica Acta (BBA)-Molecular Cell Research","volume":"1867","number":"7","pages":"118673","year":"2020","publisher":"Elsevier"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Friedl2004","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"The movement of cells that maintain cell-cell junctions yet protrude along or within tissues is an important mechanism for cell positioning in morphogenesis, tissue repair and cancer. Collective cell migration shares similarities but also important differences to individually migrating cells. Coherent groups of cells are arranged and held together by cell-cell adhesion molecules, including cadherins, integrins, ALCAM and NCAM. Integrins of the $\\beta$1 and $\\beta$3 families further provide polarized interactions with the extracellular tissue environment, while matrix-degrading proteases become focalized to substrate contacts to widen tissue space for the advancing cell mass. By generating one functional unit, in contrast to individual cell migration, collective migration provides the active and passive translocation of mobile and non-mobile cells, respectively. This review highlights cellular and molecular principles of collective migration in the context of morphogenic tissue patterning and tumor cell invasion.","author":"Friedl, Peter and Hegerfeldt, Yael and Tusch, Miriam","booktitle":"Int. J. Dev. Biol.","doi":"10.1387/ijdb.041821pf","issn":"02146282","keywords":"Cadherin,Cell migration,Collagen,Epithelial cell,Integrin,MMP","pmid":"15349818","title":"{Collective cell migration in morphogenesis and cancer}","year":"2004"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Fuchizaki1995","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"58fc81321c756f4a","fields":{"title":"Computer modelling of three-dimensional cellular pattern growth","author":"Fuchizaki, Kazuhiro and Kusaba, Takuo and Kawasaki, Kyozi","journal":"Philosophical Magazine B","volume":"71","number":"3","pages":"333--357","year":"1995","publisher":"Taylor \\& Francis"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Ganca2021","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Large-scale tissue deformation during biological processes such as morphogenesis requires cellular rearrangements. The simplest rearrangement in confluent cellular monolayers involves neighbor exchanges among four cells, called a T1 transition, in analogy to foams. But unlike foams, cells must execute a sequence of molecular processes, such as endocytosis of adhesion molecules, to complete a T1 transition. Such processes could take a long time compared to other timescales in the tissue. In this work, we incorporate this idea by augmenting vertex models to require a fixed, finite time for T1 transitions, which we call the “T1 delay time”. We study how variations in T1 delay time affect tissue mechanics, by quantifying the relaxation time of tissues in the presence of T1 delays and comparing that to the cell-shape based timescale that characterizes fluidity in the absence of any T1 delays. We show that the molecular-scale T1 delay timescale dominates over the cell shape-scale collective response timescale when the T1 delay time is the larger of the two. We extend this analysis to tissues that become anisotropic under convergent extension, finding similar results. Moreover, we find that increasing the T1 delay time increases the percentage of higher-fold coordinated vertices and rosettes, and decreases the overall number of successful T1s, contributing to a more elastic-like—and less fluid-like—tissue response. Our work suggests that molecular mechanisms that act as a brake on T1 transitions could stiffen global tissue mechanics and enhance rosette formation during morphogenesis.","archiveprefix":"arXiv","arxivid":"2102.06975","author":"Erdemci-Tandogan, Gonca and {Lisa Manning}, M.","doi":"10.1371/journal.pcbi.1009049","eprint":"2102.06975","file":":C\\:/Users/Tao/AppData/Local/Mendeley Ltd./Mendeley Desktop/Downloaded/Erdemci-Tandogan, Lisa Manning - 2021 - Effect of cellular rearrangement time delays on the rheology of vertex models for confluent tiss.pdf:pdf","isbn":"1111111111","issn":"15537358","journal":"PLoS Computational Biology","mendeley-groups":"SJTU/JM Schwarz/3D Vertex Model,SJTU/JM Schwarz/references","number":"6","pages":"1--18","pmid":"34097706","title":"{Effect of cellular rearrangement time delays on the rheology of vertex models for confluent tissues}","url":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1371/journal.pcbi.1009049","volume":"17","year":"2021"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Gandikota2021","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"cd5ece175edb7cd6","fields":{"author":"Gandikota, M. C. and Schwarz, J. M.","title":"Buckling without bending morphogenesis: nonlinearities, spatial confinement, and branching hierarchies","year":"2021","journal":"New J. Phys.","volume":"23","pages":"063060"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Giavazzi2018","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"71b5be44ef72177c","fields":{"title":"Flocking transitions in confluent tissues","author":"Giavazzi, Fabio and Paoluzzi, Matteo and Macchi, Marta and Bi, Dapeng and Scita, Giorgio and Manning, M. Lisa and Cerbino, Roberto and Marchetti, M. Cristina","journal":"Soft Matter","volume":"14","number":"18","pages":"3471--3477","year":"2018","publisher":"Royal Society of Chemistry"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Gjorevski2022","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"b3c71226c364b886","fields":{"title":"Tissue geometry drives deterministic organoid patterning","author":"Gjorevski, N. and Nikolaev, M. and Brown, T. E. and Mitrofanova, O. and Brandenberg, N. and DelRio, F. W. and Yavitt, F. M. and Liberali, P. and Anseth, K. S. and Lutolf, M. P.","journal":"Science","volume":"375","number":"6576","pages":"eaaw9021","year":"2022","publisher":"American Association for the Advancement of Science"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Glazier2005","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"7ac215350b232982","fields":{"title":"On multiscale approaches to three-dimensional modelling of morphogenesis","author":"Chaturvedi, Rajiv and Huang, Chengbang and Kazmierczak, Bogdan and Schneider, T. and Izaguirre, Jesus A. and Glimm, Tilmann and Hentschel, H. George E. and Glazier, J. A. and Newman, S. A. and Alber, M. S.","journal":"Journal of the Royal Society Interface","volume":"2","number":"3","pages":"237--253","year":"2005","publisher":"The Royal Society London"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Golloshi2022","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"c627d5a0890cf98b","fields":{"title":"Constricted migration is associated with stable 3D genome structure differences in cancer cells","author":"Golloshi, Rosela and Playter, Christopher and Freeman, Trevor F and Das, Priyojit and Raines, Thomas Isaac and Garretson, Joshua H and Thurston, Delaney and McCord, Rachel Patton","journal":"EMBO reports","volume":"23","number":"10","pages":"e52149","year":"2022"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Gotz2005","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"ec14193eec4fb494","fields":{"title":"The cell biology of neurogenesis","author":"G{\\\"o}tz, Magdalena and Huttner, Wieland B","journal":"Nature reviews Molecular cell biology","volume":"6","number":"10","pages":"777--788","year":"2005","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group UK London"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Graner1992","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"An amorphous carbon network generated by quenching high-density high-temperature liquid carbon using tight-binding molecular-dynamics simulation is found to contain a large fraction of tetrahedral bonding sites. Investigations of the structural, vibrational, and electronic properties of the theoretical model provide fresh insights into the microscopic structure of diamondlike amorphous carbon films produced in ion-beam deposition experiments.","author":"Graner, Fran{\\c{c}}ois and Glazier, James A.","isbn":"0031-9007$\\backslash$n1079-7114","issn":"00319007","journal":"Phys. Rev. Lett.","volume":"69","pages":"213-216","pmid":"10054179","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.69.2013","title":"{Simulation of biological cell sorting using a two-dimensional extended Potts model}","year":"1992"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Greiner2013","verdict":"unverified","source":"none","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"","fields":{"author":"Greiner, A. M. and Chen, H. and Spatz, J. P. and Kemkemer, R.","title":"Cyclic tensile strain controls cell shape and directs active stress fiber formation and focal adhesion alignment in spreading cells","year":"2013","journal":"PLoS ONE","volume":"8","pages":"e77328"},"diffs":[],"failure_note":"not found at the registries — books published before DOIs often are not registered; the entry may still be correct"},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Grosser2021","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"e10641d52377a4e2","fields":{"title":"Cell and nucleus shape as an indicator of tissue fluidity in carcinoma","author":"Grosser, Steffen and Lippoldt, J{\\\"u}rgen and Oswald, Linda and Merkel, Matthias and Sussman, Daniel M. and Renner, Fr{\\'e}d{\\'e}ric and Gottheil, Pablo and Morawetz, Erik W. and Fuhs, Thomas and Xie, Xiaofan and others","journal":"Physical Review X","volume":"11","number":"1","pages":"011033","year":"2021","publisher":"APS"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Gupta2021","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"b7a952318ed3d576","fields":{"title":"The role of vimentin--nuclear interactions in persistent cell motility through confined spaces","author":"Gupta, Sarthak and Patteson, Alison E and Schwarz, JM","journal":"New journal of physics","volume":"23","number":"9","pages":"093042","year":"2021","publisher":"IOP Publishing"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Harada2014","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"ab6cbd801d8e7e38","fields":{"title":"Nuclear lamin stiffness is a barrier to 3D migration, but softness can limit survival","author":"Harada, Takamasa and Swift, Joe and Irianto, Jerome and Shin, Jae-Won and Spinler, Kyle R and Athirasala, Avathamsa and Diegmiller, Rocky and Dingal, PC Dave P and Ivanovska, Irena L and Discher, Dennis E","journal":"Journal of Cell Biology","volume":"204","number":"5","pages":"669--682","year":"2014","publisher":"The Rockefeller University Press"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Harris1976b","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"The differential adhesion hypothesis, developed by Malcolm Steinberg, proposes that the histotypic sorting out behavior of aggregated cells is mechanistically equivalent to certain aspects of liquid surface tension, specifically the spontaneous separation of immiscible liquids of differing surface tension. According to Steinberg's hypothesis, the adhesive forces between aggregated cells play essentially the same role in cell sorting as are played by intermolecular attractive forces in liquid surface tension. In this paper I discuss a number of crucial distinctions between intermolecular attraction (in liquids) and intercellular adhesion (in aggregates). First, liquid drops are closed systems thermodynamically whereas aggregates of living cells can generate an indeterminate amount of metabolic energy capable of altering cell positions and adhesions. Secondly, intercellular adhesions are more than just close range attractions since cells can be held together by forces in addition to those which originally pulled them together. Third, the breakage of intercellular adhesions need not be simply the reverse, thermodynamically, of the formation of those adhesions. And fourthly, because intercellular adhesion is generally concentrated at relatively small foci such as desmosomes, a maximization of intercellular adhesion does not necessarily require a maximization of intercellular contact area, or vice versa. In addition, several alternative hypotheses are proposed, each of which is theoretically capable of explaining cell sorting and the other surface tension-like aspects of cell aggregate behavior which Steinberg has sought to explain as consequences of differential adhesion. In particular, a differential surface contraction hypothesis is proposed, according to which cell sorting and related phenomena are the results of cell surface contractions induced to occur over those portions of the cell surface which are exposed to the surrounding culture medium. Because of the evidence that various invagination type movements of embryonic epithelia are caused by cell surface contractions, it is suggested that differential surface contraction is the most likely explanation of histotypic cell sorting. A number of experiments are suggested by which these various hypotheses might be tested. {\\textcopyright} 1976.","author":"Harris, Albert K.","doi":"10.1016/0022-5193(76)90019-9","issn":"10958541","journal":"J. Theor. Biol.","title":"{Is cell sorting caused by differences in the work of intercellular adhesion? A critique of the steinberg hypothesis}","year":"1976"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Heuer2023","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"10776b6d162cf6fe","fields":{"title":"Diversity and evolution of cerebellar folding in mammals","author":"Heuer, Katja and Traut, Nicolas and de Sousa, Alexandra Allison and Valk, Sofie Louise and Clavel, Julien and Toro, Roberto","journal":"Elife","volume":"12","pages":"e85907","year":"2023","publisher":"eLife Sciences Publications Limited"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Hilgenfeldt2008","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"In multicellular organisms, cells pack together to form tissues of intricate and well defined morphology. How such cell-packing geometries arise is an important open question in biology, because the functionality of many differentiated tissues depends on their reliable formation. We show that combining adhesive forces due to E- and N-cadherin with a quantitative description of cell membrane elasticity in an interfacial energy model explains not only the qualitative neighbor relations, but also the detailed geometry of a tissue. The characteristic cellular geometries in the eyes of both wild-type Drosophila and genetic mutants are accurately reproduced by using a fixed set of few, physically motivated parameters. The model predicts adhesion strengths in the eye epithelium, quantifies their role relative to membrane elasticity, and reveals how simple minimization of interfacial energy can give rise to complex geometric patterns of important biological functionality.","author":"Hilgenfeldt, S. and Erisken, S. and Carthew, R. W.","doi":"10.1073/pnas.0711077105","issn":"0027-8424","journal":"Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci.","title":"{Physical modeling of cell geometric order in an epithelial tissue}","year":"2008"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Hochner2012","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"177b06b816cb8f3b","fields":{"title":"An embodied view of octopus neurobiology","author":"Hochner, Binyamin","journal":"Current biology","volume":"22","number":"20","pages":"R887--R892","year":"2012","publisher":"Elsevier"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Hoencamp2021","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"50d125af23e09a49","fields":{"title":"3D genomics across the tree of life reveals condensin II as a determinant of architecture type","author":"Hoencamp, Claire and Dudchenko, Olga and Elbatsh, Ahmed MO and Brahmachari, Sumitabha and Raaijmakers, Jonne A and van Schaik, Tom and Sede{\\~n}o Cacciatore, {\\'A}ngela and Contessoto, Vin{\\'\\i}cius G and van Heesbeen, Roy GHP and van den Broek, Bram and others","journal":"Science","volume":"372","number":"6545","pages":"984--989","year":"2021","publisher":"American Association for the Advancement of Science"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Hofer2021","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"c2077043c5ef4b7b","fields":{"title":"Engineering organoids","author":"Hofer, Moritz and Lutolf, Matthias P.","journal":"Nature Reviews Materials","volume":"6","number":"5","pages":"402--420","year":"2021","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Hoffman2015","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"4cd4956b7b224856","fields":{"title":"Towards a dynamic understanding of cadherin-based mechanobiology","author":"Hoffman, Brenton D. and Yap, Alpha S.","journal":"Trends in Cell Biology","volume":"25","number":"12","pages":"803--814","year":"2015","publisher":"Elsevier"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Honda1978","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"925c9a31a06e62dd","fields":{"title":"Description of cellular patterns by Dirichlet domains: the two-dimensional case.","author":"Honda, H.","journal":"J. Theor. Biol.","volume":"72","pages":"523--543","year":"1978"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Honda1982","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"2bc4bac47130ba68","fields":{"title":"Cell movements in a living mammalian tissue: long-term observation of individual cells in wounded corneal endothelia of cats","author":"Honda, H. and Ogita, Y. and Higuchi, S. and Kani, K.","journal":"Journal of Morphology","volume":"174","number":"1","pages":"25--39","year":"1982","publisher":"Wiley Online Library"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Honda2004","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"8f0e73ea3ad8e533","fields":{"title":"A three-dimensional vertex dynamics cell model of space-filling polyhedra simulating cell behavior in a cell aggregate","author":"Honda, Hisao and Tanemura, Masaharu and Nagai, Tatsuzo","journal":"Journal of Theoretical Biology","volume":"226","number":"4","pages":"439--453","year":"2004","publisher":"Elsevier"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Humphrey2014","verdict":"unverified","source":"none","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"","fields":{"author":"Humphrey, J. D. and Dufresne, E. R. and Schwartz, M. A.","title":"Mechanotransduction and extracellular homeostasis","year":"2014","journal":"Nat. Rev. Mol. Cell Biol.","volume":"15","pages":"802-812"},"diffs":[],"failure_note":"not found at the registries — books published before DOIs often are not registered; the entry may still be correct"},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Ilina2020","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"eb73918e5661fc96","fields":{"title":"Cell--cell adhesion and 3D matrix confinement determine jamming transitions in breast cancer invasion","author":"Ilina, Olga and Gritsenko, Pavlo G. and Syga, Simon and Lippoldt, J{\\\"u}rgen and La Porta, Caterina A. M. and Chepizhko, Oleksandr and Grosser, Steffen and Vullings, Manon and Bakker, Gert-Jan and Starru{\\ss}, J{\\\"o}rn and others","journal":"Nature Cell Biology","volume":"22","number":"9","pages":"1103--1115","year":"2020","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Jiang1998","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"In the mound stage of Dictyostelium discoideum, pre-stalk cells sort and form a tip at the apex. How this pattern forms is as yet unknown. A cellular level model allows us to simulate both differential cell adhesion and chemotaxis, to show that with differential adhesion only, pre-stalk cells move to the surface of the mound but form no tip. With chemotaxis driven by an outgoing circular wave only, a tip forms but contains both pre-stalk and pre-spore cells. Only for a narrow range of relative strengths between differential adhesion and chemotaxis can both mechanisms work in concert to form a tip containing only pre-stalk cells. The simulations provide a method to determine the processes necessary for patterning and suggest a series of further experiments.","author":"Jiang, Yi and Levine, Herbert and Glazier, James","doi":"10.1016/S0006-3495(98)77707-0","issn":"00063495","journal":"Biophys. J.","title":"{Possible cooperation of differential adhesion and chemotaxis in mound formation of Dictyostelium}","year":"1998"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Kaliman2016","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"c476019c565ee772","fields":{"author":"Kaliman, Sara and Jayachandran, Christina and Rehfeldt, Florian and Smith, Ana-Sunčana","title":"Limits of Applicability of the Voronoi Tessellation Determined by Centers of Cell Nuclei to Epithelium Morphology","journal":"Frontiers in Physiology","volume":"7","pages":"551","year":"2016"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Karzbrun2018","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"d700bda90a84c7a8","fields":{"title":"Human brain organoids on a chip reveal the physics of folding","author":"Karzbrun, Eyal and Kshirsagar, Aditya and Cohen, Sidney R and Hanna, Jacob H and Reiner, Orly","journal":"Nature Physics","volume":"14","number":"5","pages":"515--522","year":"2018","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Katava2022","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"23fa51b5d9c9c166","fields":{"title":"Chromatin dynamics controls epigenetic domain formation","author":"Katava, Marina and Shi, Guang and Thirumalai, D","journal":"Biophysical Journal","volume":"121","number":"15","pages":"2895--2905","year":"2022","publisher":"Elsevier"},"diffs":[{"field":"year","in_document":"2022","in_source":"2021","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Kesavan2020","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"The formation and maintenance of sharp boundaries between groups of cells play a vital role during embryonic development as they serve to compartmentalize cells with similar fates. Some of these boundaries also act as organizers, with the ability to induce specific cell fates and morphogenesis in the surrounding cells. The midbrain-hindbrain boundary (MHB) is such an organizer: it acts as a lineage restriction boundary to prevent the intermingling of cells with different developmental fates. However, the mechanisms underlying the lineage restriction process remain unclear. Here, using novel fluorescent knock-in reporters, live imaging, Cre/lox-mediated lineage tracing, atomic force microscopy-based cell adhesion assays and mutant analysis, we analyze the process of lineage restriction at the MHB and provide mechanistic details. Specifically, we show that lineage restriction occurs by the end of gastrulation, and that the subsequent formation of sharp gene expression boundaries in the developing MHB occur through complementary mechanisms, i.e. cell-fate plasticity and cell sorting. Furthermore, we show that cell sorting at the MHB involves differential adhesion among midbrain and hindbrain cells that is mediated by N-cadherin and Eph-ephrin signaling.","author":"Kesavan, Gokul and Machate, Anja and Hans, Stefan and Brand, Michael","doi":"10.1242/dev.186882","issn":"14779129","journal":"Development","keywords":"Cell adhesion,Cell sorting,Ephrin signaling,Midbrain-hindbrain boundary (MHB),Zebrafish","pmid":"32439756","title":"{Cell-fate plasticity, adhesion and cell sorting complementarily establish a sharp midbrain-hindbrain boundary}","year":"2020"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Khan2014","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Understanding the cellular and mechanical processes that underlie the shape changes of individual cells and their collective behaviors in a tissue during dynamic and complex morphogenetic events is currently one of the major frontiers in developmental biology. The advent of highspeed time-lapse microscopy and its use in monitoring the cellular events in fluorescently labeled developing organisms demonstrate tremendous promise in establishing detailed descriptions of these events and could potentially provide a foundation for subsequent hypothesis-driven research strategies.However, obtaining quantitative measurements of dynamic shapes and behaviors of cells and tissues in a rapidly developing metazoan embryo using time-lapse 3D microscopy remains technically challenging, with the main hurdle being the shortage of robust imaging processing and analysis tools.We have developed EDGE4D, a software tool for segmenting and tracking membrane-labeled cells using multi-photon microscopy data. Our results demonstrate that EDGE4D enables quantification of the dynamics of cell shape changes, cell interfaces and neighbor relations at single-cell resolution during a complex epithelial folding event in the early Drosophila embryo.We expect this tool to be broadly useful for the analysis of epithelial cell geometries andmovements in a wide variety of developmental contexts. {\\textcopyright} 2014. Published by The Company of Biologists Ltd.","author":"Khan, Zia and Wang, Yu Chiun and Wieschaus, Eric F. and Kaschube, Matthias","doi":"10.1242/dev.107730","issn":"14779129","journal":"Dev.","keywords":"Cell shape analysis,Cell shape reconstruction,Cell tracking,Drosophila melanogaster,Epithelial folding,Live imaging","title":"{Quantitative 4D analyses of epithelial folding during Drosophila gastrulation}","year":"2014"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Kim2021","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"f47e8dd2b7323c89","fields":{"title":"Embryonic tissues as active foams","author":"Kim, Sangwoo and Pochitaloff, Marie and Stooke-Vaughan, Georgina A. and Camp{\\`a}s, Otger","journal":"Nature Physics","volume":"17","number":"7","pages":"859--866","year":"2021","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group"},"diffs":[{"field":"year","in_document":"2021","in_source":"2020","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Kim2022","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"622d10c709dccfdf","fields":{"title":"A nuclear jamming transition in vertebrate organogenesis","author":"Kim, Sangwoo and Amini, Rana and Camp{\\`a}s, Otger","journal":"BioRxiv","pages":"2022--07","year":"2022","publisher":"Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Klopper2010","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Cell sorting is a widespread phenomenon pivotal to the early development of multicellular organisms. In vitro cell sorting studies have been instrumental in revealing the cellular properties driving this process. However, these studies have as yet been limited to two-dimensional analysis of three-dimensional cell sorting events. Here we describe a method to record the sorting of primary zebrafish ectoderm and mesoderm germ layer progenitor cells in three dimensions over time, and quantitatively analyze their sorting behavior using an order parameter related to heterotypic interface length. We investigate the cell population size dependence of sorted aggregates and find that the germ layer progenitor cells engulfed in the final configuration display a relationship between total interfacial length and system size according to a simple geometrical argument, subject to a finite-size effect. {\\textcopyright} 2010 EDP Sciences, SIF, Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg.","author":"Klopper, A. V. and Krens, G. and Grill, S. W. and Heisenberg, C. P.","doi":"10.1140/epje/i2010-10642-y","issn":"12928941","journal":"Eur. Phys. J. E","title":"{Finite-size corrections to scaling behavior in sorted cell aggregates}","year":"2010"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Kosmrlj2017","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"49dbed46117b589d","fields":{"title":"Statistical mechanics of thin spherical shells","author":"Ko{\\v{s}}mrlj, Andrej and Nelson, David R","journal":"Physical Review X","volume":"7","number":"1","pages":"011002","year":"2017","publisher":"APS"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Krajnc2018","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"d0cae1255326d02f","fields":{"title":"Fluidization of epithelial sheets by active cell rearrangements","author":"Krajnc, Matej and Dasgupta, Sabyasachi and Ziherl, Primo{\\v{z}} and Prost, Jacques","journal":"Physical Review E","volume":"98","number":"2","pages":"022409","year":"2018","publisher":"APS"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Lammerding2011","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"69b5d1cf02139f68","fields":{"title":"Mechanics of the Nucleus","author":"Lammerding, J.","journal":"Compr. Physiol.","volume":"1","issue":"2","pages":"783","numpages":"24","year":"2011"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Lancaster2013","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"5133004d3a0087bc","fields":{"author":"Lancaster, M. A. and Renner, M. and Martin, C.-A. and Wenzel, D. and Bicknell, L. S. and Hurles, M. E. and Homfray, T. and Penninger, J. M. and Jackson, A. P. and Knoblich, J. A.","title":"Cerebral organoids model human brain development and microcephaly","year":"2013","journal":"Nature","volume":"501","number":"6","pages":"373-379"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Lancaster2014","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"3f06b5cef8a7b83f","fields":{"title":"Organogenesis in a dish: modeling development and disease using organoid technologies","author":"Lancaster, Madeline A. and Knoblich, Juergen A.","journal":"Science","volume":"345","number":"6194","pages":"1247125","year":"2014","publisher":"American Association for the Advancement of Science"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Lancaster2014b","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0cb6fd1df6ec08e2","fields":{"title":"Generation of cerebral organoids from human pluripotent stem cells","author":"Lancaster, Madeline A. and Knoblich, Juergen A.","journal":"Nature Protocols","volume":"9","number":"10","pages":"2329--2340","year":"2014","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Lancaster2021","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"4d7d93acc953e087","fields":{"title":"An early cell shape transition drives evolutionary expansion of the human forebrain","journal":"Cell","author":"Silvia Benito-Kwiecinski and Stefano L. Giandomenico and Magdalena Sutcliffe and Erlend S. Riis and Paula Freire-Pritchett and Iva Kelava and Stephanie Wunderlich and Ulrich Martin and Gregory A. Wray and Kate McDole and Madeline A. Lancaster","volume":"184","number":"8","pages":"2084-2102.e19","year":"2021"},"diffs":[{"field":"authors","in_document":"Silvia Benito-Kwiecinski and Stefano L. Giandomenico and Magdalena Sutcliffe and Erlend S. Riis and Paula Freire-Pritchett and Iva Kelava and Stephanie Wunderlich and Ulrich Martin and Gregory A. Wray and Kate McDole and Madeline A. Lancaster","in_source":"Silvia Benito-Kwiecinski and Stefano L. Giandomenico and Magdalena Sutcliffe and Erlend S. Riis and Paula Freire-Pritchett and Iva Kelava and Stephanie Wunderlich and Ulrich Martin and Greg Wray and Madeline A. Lancaster","severity":"high"},{"field":"year","in_document":"2021","in_source":"2020","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Landsberg2009a","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Subdividing proliferating tissues into compartments is an evolutionarily conserved strategy of animal development [1-6]. Signals across boundaries between compartments can result in local expression of secreted proteins organizing growth and patterning of tissues [1-6]. Sharp and straight interfaces between compartments are crucial for stabilizing the position of such organizers and therefore for precise implementation of body plans. Maintaining boundaries in proliferating tissues requires mechanisms to counteract cell rearrangements caused by cell division; however, the nature of such mechanisms remains unclear. Here we quantitatively analyzed cell morphology and the response to the laser ablation of cell bonds in the vicinity of the anteroposterior compartment boundary in developing Drosophila wings. We found that mechanical tension is approximately 2.5-fold increased on cell bonds along this compartment boundary as compared to the remaining tissue. Cell bond tension is decreased in the presence of Y-27632 [7], an inhibitor of Rho-kinase whose main effector is Myosin II [8]. Simulations using a vertex model [9] demonstrate that a 2.5-fold increase in local cell bond tension suffices to guide the rearrangement of cells after cell division to maintain compartment boundaries. Our results provide a physical mechanism in which the local increase in Myosin II-dependent cell bond tension directs cell sorting at compartment boundaries. {\\textcopyright} 2009 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.","author":"Landsberg, Katharina P. and Farhadifar, Reza and Ranft, Jonas and Umetsu, Daiki and Widmann, Thomas J. and Bittig, Thomas and Said, Amani and J{\\\"{u}}licher, Frank and Dahmann, Christian","doi":"10.1016/j.cub.2009.10.021","issn":"09609822","journal":"Curr. Biol.","keywords":"CELLBIO,DEVBIO","title":"{Increased Cell Bond Tension Governs Cell Sorting at the Drosophila Anteroposterior Compartment Boundary}","year":"2009"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Larsell","verdict":"unverified","source":"none","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"","fields":{"title":"The Comparative Anatomy and Histology of the Cerebellum","author":"Larsell, Olof","year":"1967","publisher":"University of Minnesota Press"},"diffs":[],"failure_note":"not found at the registries — books published before DOIs often are not registered; the entry may still be correct"},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Lawton2019","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"d0bbed4865547bc7","fields":{"title":"Cerebellar folding is initiated by mechanical constraints on a fluid-like layer without a cellular pre-pattern","author":"Lawton, Andrew K and Engstrom, Tyler and Rohrbach, Daniel and Omura, Masaaki and Turnbull, Daniel H and Mamou, Jonathan and Zhang, Teng and Schwarz, Jennifer M and Joyner, Alexandra L","journal":"Elife","volume":"8","pages":"e45019","year":"2019","publisher":"eLife Sciences Publications Limited"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Lejeune2016","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"82934b31735c635f","fields":{"author":"Lejeune, Emma and Javili, Ali and Weickenmeier, Johannes and Kuhl, Ellen and Linder, Christian","title":"Tri-layer wrinkling as a mechanism for anchoring center initiation in the developing cerebellum","journal":"Soft Matter","year":"2016","volume":"12","issue":"25","pages":"5613-5620","publisher":"The Royal Society of Chemistry"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Lejeune2019","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"813266c631daa537","fields":{"author":"Lejeune, Emma and Dortdivanlioglu, Berkin and Kuhl, Ellen and Linder, Christian","title":"Understanding the mechanical link between oriented cell division and cerebellar morphogenesis","journal":"Soft Matter","year":"2019","volume":"15","issue":"10","pages":"2204-2215","publisher":"The Royal Society of Chemistry"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Lele2018","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"a5ef31571e585102","fields":{"title":"Mechanical principles of nuclear shaping and positioning","author":"Lele, Tanmay P and Dickinson, Richard B and Gundersen, Gregg G","journal":"Journal of Cell Biology","volume":"217","number":"10","pages":"3330--3342","year":"2018","publisher":"Rockefeller University Press"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Lieberman2009","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"a611d90c017b046a","fields":{"title":"Comprehensive mapping of long-range interactions reveals folding principles of the human genome","author":"Lieberman-Aiden, Erez and Van Berkum, Nynke L and Williams, Louise and Imakaev, Maxim and Ragoczy, Tobias and Telling, Agnes and Amit, Ido and Lajoie, Bryan R and Sabo, Peter J and Dorschner, Michael O and others","journal":"science","volume":"326","number":"5950","pages":"289--293","year":"2009","publisher":"American Association for the Advancement of Science"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Liu2021","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"title":"Dynamic Nuclear Structure Emerges from Chromatin Cross-Links and Motors","author":"Liu, Kuang and Patteson, Alison E. and Banigan, Edward J. and Schwarz, J. M.","journal":"Phys. Rev. Lett.","volume":"126","issue":"15","pages":"158101","numpages":"6","year":"2021","month":"Apr","publisher":"American Physical Society","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.126.158101","url":"https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevLett.126.158101"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Lomakin2020","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"1e09d0090c241bff","fields":{"title":"The nucleus acts as a ruler tailoring cell responses to spatial constraints","author":"Lomakin, AJ and Cattin, CJ and Cuvelier, Damien and Alraies, Zahraa and Molina, Marc and Nader, GPF and Srivastava, Nishit and S{\\'a}ez, PJ and Garcia-Arcos, JM and Zhitnyak, IY and others","journal":"Science","volume":"370","number":"6514","pages":"eaba2894","year":"2020","publisher":"American Association for the Advancement of Science"},"diffs":[{"field":"authors","in_document":"Lomakin, AJ and Cattin, CJ and Cuvelier, Damien and Alraies, Zahraa and Molina, Marc and Nader, GPF and Srivastava, Nishit and S{\\'a}ez, PJ and Garcia-Arcos, JM and Zhitnyak, IY and others","in_source":"A.J. Lomakin and C.J. Cattin and D. Cuvelier and Z. Alraies and M. Molina and G. Nader and N. Srivastava and J.M. Garcia-Arcos and I.Y. Zhitnyak and A. Bhargava and M.K. Driscoll and E.S. Welf and R. Fiolka and R.J. Petrie and N. Manel and A.M. Lennon-Duménil and D.J. Müller and M. Piel","severity":"high"},{"field":"year","in_document":"2020","in_source":"2019","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Luca2022","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"740c2ca091453467","fields":{"abstract":"Biological processes such as embryogenesis, wound healing and cancer progression, crucially rely on the ability of epithelial cells to coordinate their mechanical activity over length scales order of magnitudes larger than the typical cellular size. While regulated by signalling pathways, such as YAP (yes-associated protein), MAPK (mitogen-activated protein kinase) and Wnt, this behavior is believed to additionally hinge on a minimal toolkit of physical mechanisms, of which liquid crystal order is the most promising candidate. Yet, experimental and theoretical studies have given so far inconsistent results in this respect: whereas nematic order is often invoked in the interpretation of experimental data, computational models have instead suggested that hexatic order could in fact emerge in the biologically relevant region of parameter space. In this article we resolve this dilemma. Using a combination of in vitro experiments on Madin-Darby canine kidney cells (MDCK), numerical simulations and analytical work, we demonstrate that both nematic and hexatic order is in fact present in epithelial layers, with the former being dominant at large length scales and the latter at small length scales. In MDCK GII cells on uncoated glass, these different types of liquid crystal order crossover at $34 \\mu$m, corresponding approximatively to clusters of $21$ cells. Our work sheds light on the emergent organization of living matter, provides a new set of tools for analyzing the structure of epithelia and paves the way toward a comprehensive and predictive mesoscopic theory of tissues.","archiveprefix":"arXiv","arxivid":"2202.00668","author":"Armengol-Collado, Josep-Maria and Carenza, Livio Nicola and Eckert, Julia and Krommydas, Dimitris and Giomi, Luca","eprint":"2202.00668","file":":C\\:/Users/Tao/AppData/Local/Mendeley Ltd./Mendeley Desktop/Downloaded/Armengol-Collado et al. - 2022 - Epithelia are multiscale active liquid crystals.pdf:pdf","mendeley-groups":"SJTU/JM Schwarz/3D Vertex Model,SJTU/JM Schwarz/references","title":"{Epithelia are multiscale active liquid crystals}","url":"http://arxiv.org/abs/2202.00668","year":"2022"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Lucarini2009","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"We perturb the simple cubic (SC), body-centered cubic (BCC), and face-centered cubic (FCC) structures with a spatial Gaussian noise whose adimensional strength is controlled by the parameter $\\alpha$ and analyze the statistical properties of the cells of the resulting Voronoi tessellations using an ensemble approach. We concentrate on topological properties of the cells, such as the number of faces, and on metric properties of the cells, such as the area, volume and the isoperimetric quotient. The topological properties of the Voronoi tessellations of the SC and FCC crystals are unstable with respect to the introduction of noise, because the corresponding polyhedra are geometrically degenerate, whereas the tessellation of the BCC crystal is topologically stable even against noise of small but finite intensity. Whereas the average volume of the cells is the intensity parameter of the system and does not depend on the noise, the average area of the cells has a rather interesting behavior with respect to noise intensity. For weak noise, the mean area of the Voronoi tessellations corresponding to perturbed BCC and FCC perturbed increases quadratically with the noise intensity. In the case of perturbed SCC crystals, there is an optimal amount of noise that minimizes the mean area of the cells. Already for a moderate amount of noise ($\\alpha${\\textgreater}0.5), the statistical properties of the three perturbed tessellations are indistinguishable, and for intense noise ($\\alpha${\\textgreater}2), results converge to those of the Poisson-Voronoi tessellation. Notably, 2-parameter gamma distributions constitute an excellent model for the empirical pdf of all considered topological and metric properties. By analyzing jointly the statistical properties of the area and of the volume of the cells, we discover that also the cells shape, measured by the isoperimetric quotient, fluctuates. The Voronoi tessellations of the BCC and of the FCC structures result to be local maxima for the isoperimetric quotient among space-filling tessellations, which suggests a weaker form of the recently disproved Kelvin conjecture. Moreover, whereas the size of the isoperimetric quotient fluctuations go to zero linearly with noise in the SC and BCC case, the decrease is quadratic in the FCC case. Correspondingly, anomalous scaling relations with exponents larger than 3/2 are observed between the area and the volumes of the cells for all cases considered, and, except for the FCC structure, also for infinitesimal noise. In the Poisson-Voronoi limit, the exponent is 1.67. The anomaly in the scaling indicates that large cells preferentially feature large isoperimetric quotients. The FCC structure, in spite of being topologically unstable, results to be the most stable against noise when the shape-as measured by the isoperimetric quotient-of the cells is considered. These scaling relations apply only for a finite range and should be taken as descriptive of the bulk statistical properties of the cells. As the number of faces is strongly correlated with the sphericity (cells with more faces are bulkier), the anomalous scaling is heavily reduced when we perform power law fits separately on cells with a specific number of faces. {\\textcopyright} 2008 Springer Science+Business Media, LLC.","author":"Lucarini, Valerio","doi":"10.1007/s10955-008-9668-y","issn":"00224715","journal":"J. Stat. Phys.","keywords":"Anomalous scaling,Body-centered cubic,Cubic crystals,Face-centered cubic,Gamma distribution,Gaussian noise,Numerical simulations,Poisson point process,Random geometry,Simple cubic,Symmetry break,Topological stability,Voronoi tessellation","title":"{Three-dimensional random voronoi tessellations: From cubic crystal lattices to poisson point processes}","year":"2009"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Maeshima2018","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"dfedc316152a2bf0","fields":{"title":"Chromatin as a nuclear spring","author":"Maeshima, Kazuhiro and Tamura, Sachiko and Shimamoto, Yuta","journal":"Biophysics and Physicobiology","volume":"15","pages":"189--195","year":"2018","publisher":"The Biophysical Society of Japan"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Mahajan2022","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"d7896ed23a6de1da","fields":{"title":"Euchromatin activity enhances segregation and compaction of heterochromatin in the cell nucleus","author":"Mahajan, Achal and Yan, Wen and Zidovska, Alexandra and Saintillan, David and Shelley, Michael J","journal":"Physical Review X","volume":"12","number":"4","pages":"041033","year":"2022","publisher":"APS"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Maitre2012b","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Differential cell adhesion and cortex tension are thought to drive cell sorting by controlling cell-cell contact formation. Here, we show that cell adhesion and cortex tension have different mechanical functions in controlling progenitor cell-cell contact formation and sorting during zebrafish gastrulation. Cortex tension controls cell-cell contact expansion by modulating interfacial tension at the contact. By contrast, adhesion has little direct function in contact expansion, but instead is needed to mechanically couple the cortices of adhering cells at their contacts, allowing cortex tension to control contact expansion. The coupling function of adhesion is mediated by E-cadherin and limited by the mechanical anchoring of E-cadherin to the cortex. Thus, cell adhesion provides the mechanical scaffold for cell cortex tension to drive cell sorting during gastrulation.","author":"Ma{\\^{i}}tre, Jean L{\\'{e}}on and Berthoumieux, H{\\'{e}}l{\\`{e}}ne and Krens, Simon Frederik Gabriel and Salbreux, Guillaume and J{\\\"{u}}licher, Frank and Paluch, Ewa and Heisenberg, Carl Philipp","doi":"10.1126/science.1225399","issn":"10959203","journal":"Science (80-. ).","title":"{Adhesion functions in cell sorting by mechanically coupling the cortices of adhering cells}","year":"2012"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Majumder2011","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Domain growth during the kinetics of phase separation is studied following vapor-liquid transition in a single component Lennard-Jones fluid. Results are analyzed after appropriately mapping the continuum snapshots obtained from extensive molecular-dynamics simulations to a simple cubic lattice. For near-critical quench interconnected domain morphology is observed. A brief period of slow diffusive growth is followed by a linear viscous hydrodynamic growth that lasts for an extended period of time. This result is in contradiction with earlier reports of late-time growth exponent 1/2 that questions the uniqueness of the non-equilibrium universality for liquid-liquid and vapor-liquid transitions. {\\textcopyright} 2011 Europhysics Letters Association.","author":"Majumder, Suman and Das, Subir K.","doi":"10.1209/0295-5075/95/46002","issn":"02955075","journal":"EPL","title":"{Universality in fluid domain coarsening: The case of vapor-liquid transition}","year":"2011"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Manning2010b","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"In the course of animal morphogenesis, large-scale cell movements occur, which involve the rearrangement, mutual spreading, and compartmentalization of cell populations in specific configurations. Morphogenetic cell rearrangements such as cell sorting and mutual tissue spreading have been compared with the behaviors of immiscible liquids, which they closely resemble. Based on this similarity, it has been proposed that tissues behave as liquids and possess a characteristic surface tension, which arises as a collective, macroscopic property of groups of mobile, cohering cells. But how are tissue surface tensions generated? Different theories have been proposed to explain how mesoscopic cell properties such as cell-cell adhesion and contractility of cell interfaces may underlie tissue surface tensions. Although recent work suggests that both may be contributors, an explicit model for the dependence of tissue surface tension on these mesoscopic parameters has been missing. Here we show explicitly that the ratio of adhesion to cortical tension determines tissue surface tension. Our minimal model successfully explains the available experimental data and makes predictions, based on the feedback between mechanical energy and geometry, about the shapes of aggregate surface cells, which we verify experimentally. This model indicates that there is a crossover from adhesion dominated to cortical-tension dominated behavior as a function of the ratio between these two quantities.","author":"Manning, M. Lisa and Foty, Ramsey A. and Steinberg, Malcolm S. and Schoetz, Eva-Maria","doi":"10.1073/pnas.1003743107","issn":"0027-8424","journal":"Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci.","title":"{Coaction of intercellular adhesion and cortical tension specifies tissue surface tension}","year":"2010"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Manning2010c","verdict":"unverified","source":"none","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"","fields":{"abstract":"In the course of animal morphogenesis, large-scale cell movements occur, which involve the rearrangement, mutual spreading, and compartmentalization of cell populations in specific configurations. Morphogenetic cell rearrangements such as cell sorting and mutual tissue spreading have been compared with the behaviors of immiscible liquids, which they closely resemble. Based on this similarity, it has been proposed that tissues behave as liquids and possess a characteristic surface tension, which arises as a collective, macroscopic property of groups of mobile, cohering cells. But how are tissue surface tensions generated? Different theories have been proposed to explain how mesoscopic cell properties such as cell-cell adhesion and contractility of cell interfaces may underlie tissue surface tensions. Although recent work suggests that both may be contributors, an explicit model for the dependence of tissue surface tension on these mesoscopic parameters has been missing. Here we show explicitly that the ratio of adhesion to cortical tension determines tissue surface tension. Our minimal model successfully explains the available experimental data and makes predictions, based on the feedback between mechanical energy and geometry, about the shapes of aggregate surface cells, which we verify experimentally. This model indicates that there is a crossover from adhesion dominated to cortical-tension dominated behavior as a function of the ratio between these two quantities.","author":"Manning, M. Lisa and Foty, Ramsey A. and Steinberg, Malcolm S. and Schoetz, Eva Maria","doi":"10.1073/pnas.1003743107","issn":"00278424","journal":"Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. U. S. A.","keywords":"Cell aggregate geometry,Differential adhesion hypothesis,Differential interfacial tension hypothesis,Mathematical modeling,Self-assembly","title":"{Coaction of intercellular adhesion and cortical tension specifies tissue surface tension}","year":"2010"},"diffs":[],"failure_note":"not found at the registries — books published before DOIs often are not registered; the entry may still be correct"},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Manyuhina2014","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0e2d78e349b1678c","fields":{"title":"Elastic instabilities in a layered cerebral cortex: a revised axonal tension model for cortex folding","author":"Manyuhina, O. V. and Mayett, David and Schwarz, J. M.","journal":"New Journal of Physics","volume":"16","number":"12","pages":"123058","year":"2014","publisher":"IOP Publishing"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Marchal2022","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"68c4b42f3b8bf206","fields":{"title":"HiCRes: a computational method to estimate and predict the genomic resolution of Hi-C libraries","author":"Marchal, Claire and Singh, Nivedita and Corso-D{\\'\\i}az, Ximena and Swaroop, Anand","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","volume":"50","number":"6","pages":"e35--e35","year":"2022","publisher":"Oxford University Press"},"diffs":[{"field":"year","in_document":"2022","in_source":"2021","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Martin1975","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"8dcd455a3bb41d34","fields":{"author":"Martin, G. R. and Evans, M. J.","title":"Differentiation of clonal lines of teratocarcinoma cells: Formation of embryoid\nbodies in vitro","publisher":"","year":"1975","journal":"Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA","volume":"72","number":"","pages":"1441--1445"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Maurer2019","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"9ff05b56a76691e0","fields":{"title":"The driving force: nuclear mechanotransduction in cellular function, fate, and disease","author":"Maurer, Melanie and Lammerding, Jan","journal":"Annual review of biomedical engineering","volume":"21","pages":"443--468","year":"2019","publisher":"Annual Reviews"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Merkel2018","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"The origin of rigidity in disordered materials is an outstanding open problem in statistical physics. Previously, a class of 2D cellular models has been shown to undergo a rigidity transition controlled by a mechanical parameter that specifies cell shapes. Here, we generalize this model to 3D and find a rigidity transition that is similarly controlled by the preferred surface area S 0: the model is solid-like below a dimensionless surface area of with being the average cell volume, and fluid-like above this value. We demonstrate that, unlike jamming in soft spheres, residual stresses are necessary to create rigidity. These stresses occur precisely when cells are unable to obtain their desired geometry, and we conjecture that there is a well-defined minimal surface area possible for disordered cellular structures. We show that the behavior of this minimal surface induces a linear scaling of the shear modulus with the control parameter at the transition point, which is different from the scaling observed in particulate matter. The existence of such a minimal surface may be relevant for biological tissues and foams, and helps explain why cell shapes are a good structural order parameter for rigidity transitions in biological tissues.","archiveprefix":"arXiv","arxivid":"1706.02656","author":"Merkel, Matthias and Manning, M. Lisa","doi":"10.1088/1367-2630/aaaa13","eprint":"1706.02656","issn":"13672630","journal":"New Journal of Physics","keywords":"biological tissues,constraint counting,jamming,residual stresses,rigidity transition,solid-fluid transition,vertex model","mendeley-groups":"SJTU/JM Schwarz/BRAIN_ORGANOIDS,SJTU/JM Schwarz/3D Vertex Model,SJTU/JM Schwarz/references","number":"2","publisher":"IOP Publishing","title":"{A geometrically controlled rigidity transition in a model for confluent 3D tissues}","volume":"20","year":"2018"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Merks2005","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"author":"Merks, Roeland M.H. and Glazier, James A.","doi":"10.1016/j.physa.2004.12.028","issn":"03784371","journal":"Phys. A Stat. Mech. its Appl.","keywords":"Biological ontologies,Cell-oriented modeling,Cellular Potts Model,Computational biology,Developmental biology","title":"{A cell-centered approach to developmental biology}","year":"2005"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Mertz2012a","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"To understand how the mechanical properties of tissues emerge from interactions of multiple cells, we measure traction stresses of cohesive colonies of 1-27 cells adherent to soft substrates. We find that traction stresses are generally localized at the periphery of the colony and the total traction force scales with the colony radius. For large colony sizes, the scaling appears to approach linear, suggesting the emergence of an apparent surface tension of the order of 10 -3N/m. A simple model of the cell colony as a contractile elastic medium coupled to the substrate captures the spatial distribution of traction forces and the scaling of traction forces with the colony size. {\\textcopyright} 2012 American Physical Society.","author":"Mertz, Aaron F. and Banerjee, Shiladitya and Che, Yonglu and German, Guy K. and Xu, Ye and Hyland, Callen and Marchetti, M. Cristina and Horsley, Valerie and Dufresne, Eric R.","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.108.198101","issn":"00319007","journal":"Phys. Rev. Lett.","pmid":"23003091","title":"{Scaling of traction forces with the size of cohesive cell colonies}","year":"2012"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Mirams2013","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"93a40db1107d3a0c","fields":{"title":"Chaste: an open source C++ library for computational physiology and biology","author":"Mirams, Gary R. and Arthurs, Christopher J. and Bernabeu, Miguel O. and Bordas, Rafel and Cooper, Jonathan and Corrias, Alberto and Davit, Yohan and Dunn, Sara-Jane and Fletcher, Alexander G. and Harvey, Daniel G. and others","journal":"PLoS Computational Biology","volume":"9","number":"3","pages":"e1002970","year":"2013","publisher":"Public Library of Science San Francisco, USA"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Monier2011","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"During animal development, tissues and organs are partitioned into compartments that do not intermix. This organizing principle is essential for correct tissue morphogenesis. Given that cell sorting defects during compartmentalization in humans are thought to cause malignant invasion and congenital defects such as cranio-fronto-nasal syndrome, identifying the molecular and cellular mechanisms that keep cells apart at boundaries between compartments is important. In both vertebrates and invertebrates, transcription factors and short-range signalling pathways, such as EPH/Ephrin, Hedgehog, or Notch signalling, govern compartmental cell sorting. However, the mechanisms that mediate cell sorting downstream of these factors have remained elusive for decades. Here, we review recent data gathered in Drosophila that suggest that the generation of cortical tensile forces at compartmental boundaries by the actomyosin cytoskeleton could be a general mechanism that inhibits cell mixing between compartments. {\\textcopyright} 2011 Springer Basel AG.","author":"Monier, Bruno and P{\\'{e}}lissier-Monier, Anne and Sanson, B{\\'{e}}n{\\'{e}}dicte","booktitle":"Cell. Mol. Life Sci.","doi":"10.1007/s00018-011-0668-8","issn":"1420682X","keywords":"Actin cytoskeleton,Boundary,Cell mixing,Cell sorting,Compartment,Drosophila,Lineage restriction,Nonmuscle Myosin II","title":"{Establishment and maintenance of compartmental boundaries: Role of contractile actomyosin barriers}","year":"2011"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Montero2005","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"During vertebrate gastrulation, the three germ layers, ectoderm, mesoderm and endoderm are formed, and the resulting progenitor cells are brought into the positions from which they will later contribute more complex tissues and organs. A core element in this process is the internalization of mesodermal and endodermal progenitors at the onset of gastrulation. Although many of the molecules that induce mesendoderm have been identified, much less is known about the cellular mechanisms underlying mesendodermal cell internalization and germ layer formation. Here we show that at the onset of zebrafish gastrulation, mesendodermal progenitors in dorsal/axial regions of the germ ring internalize by single cell delamination. Once internalized, mesendodermal progenitors upregulate E-Cadherin (Cadherin 1) expression, become increasingly motile and eventually migrate along the overlying epiblast (ectodermal) cell layer towards the animal pole of the gastrula. When E-Cadherin function is compromised, mesendodermal progenitors still internalize, but, with gastrulation proceeding, fail to elongate and efficiently migrate along the epiblast, whereas epiblast cells themselves exhibit reduced radial cell intercalation movements. This indicates that cadherin-mediated cell-cell adhesion is needed within the forming shield for both epiblast cell intercalation, and mesendodermal progenitor cell elongation and migration during zebrafish gastrulation. Our data provide insight into the cellular mechanisms underlying mesendodermal progenitor cell internalization and subsequent migration during zebrafish gastrulation, and the role of cadherin-mediated cell-cell adhesion in these processes.","author":"Montero, Juan Antonio and Carvalho, Lara and Wilsch-Br{\\\"{a}}uninger, Michaela and Kilian, Beate and Mustafa, Chingdem and Heisenberg, Carl Philipp","doi":"10.1242/dev.01667","issn":"09501991","journal":"Development","keywords":"Cell migration,E-Cadherin,Gastrulation,Shield formation,Zebrafish","title":"{Shield formation at the onset of zebrafish gastrulation}","year":"2005"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Morales-Navarrete2019","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Functional tissue architecture originates by self-assembly of distinct cell types, following tissue-specific rules of cell-cell interactions. In the liver, a structural model of the lobule was pioneered by Elias in 1949. This model, however, is in contrast with the apparent random 3D arrangement of hepatocytes. Since then, no significant progress has been made to derive the organizing principles of liver tissue. To solve this outstanding problem, we computationally reconstructed 3D tissue geometry from microscopy images of mouse liver tissue and analyzed it applying soft-condensed-matter-physics concepts. Surprisingly, analysis of the spatial organization of cell polarity revealed that hepatocytes are not randomly oriented but follow a long-range liquid-crystal order. This does not depend exclusively on hepatocytes receiving instructive signals by endothelial cells, since silencing Integrin-$\\beta$1 disrupted both liquid-crystal order and organization of the sinusoidal network. Our results suggest that bi-directional communication between hepatocytes and sinusoids underlies the self-organization of liver tissue.","author":"Morales-Navarrete, Hern{\\'{a}}n and Nonaka, Hidenori and Scholich, Andr{\\'{e}} and Segovia-Miranda, Fabi{\\'{a}}n and de Back, Walter and Meyer, Kirstin and Bogorad, Roman L. and Koteliansky, Victor and Brusch, Lutz and Kalaidzidis, Yannis and J{\\\"{u}}licher, Frank and Friedrich, Benjamin M. and Zerial, Marino","doi":"10.7554/eLife.44860","issn":"2050084X","journal":"Elife","keywords":"3D tissue organization,cell polarity,liquid crystal order,liver,mouse,physics of living systems","title":"{Liquid-crystal organization of liver tissue}","year":"2019"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Mota2015","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"bb6e5e3a2ce2deed","fields":{"title":"Cortical folding scales universally with surface area and thickness, not number of neurons","author":"Mota, Bruno and Herculano-Houzel, Suzana","journal":"Science","volume":"349","number":"6243","pages":"74--77","year":"2015","publisher":"American Association for the Advancement of Science"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Nagai1990","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"bb5d61a22beb235b","fields":{"title":"Computer simulation of cellular pattern growth in two and three dimensions","author":"Nagai, Tatsuzo and Ohta, Shigetoshi and Kawasaki, Kyozi and Okuzono, Tohru","journal":"Phase Transitions: a Multinational Journal","volume":"28","number":"1-4","pages":"177--211","year":"1990","publisher":"Taylor \\& Francis"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Nagai2001","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Abstract A dynamic model is proposed for monolayered epithelial tissue, in which the monolayer is assumed to consist of prismatic cells, so that the system is described as a two-dimensional polygonal pattern. Its dynamic behaviour is determined by equations of motion for vertices in the polygonal pattern and elementary change in the system (change in the connection of vertex pairs). The vertices are driven by the sum of interfacial tension on cell boundaries and the resistance force against the deformation of cells. It is shown by computer simulations that our model possesses the characteristics of epithelial tissue, that is it has a mechanism which makes the edge number and size of a cell uniform and the shape symmetric. This mechanism finally gives rise to a regular polygonal pattern similar to a honeycomb pattern, even though initial patterns have large variations. Local equilibrium dynamics under which the size of each cell is flexibly adapted to its local environment are compared with non-local equilibrium dynamics. The local equilibrium dynamics produce more natural cellular patterns.$\\backslash$nAbstract A dynamic model is proposed for monolayered epithelial tissue, in which the monolayer is assumed to consist of prismatic cells, so that the system is described as a two-dimensional polygonal pattern. Its dynamic behaviour is determined by equations of motion for vertices in the polygonal pattern and elementary change in the system (change in the connection of vertex pairs). The vertices are driven by the sum of interfacial tension on cell boundaries and the resistance force against the deformation of cells. It is shown by computer simulations that our model possesses the characteristics of epithelial tissue, that is it has a mechanism which makes the edge number and size of a cell uniform and the shape symmetric. This mechanism finally gives rise to a regular polygonal pattern similar to a honeycomb pattern, even though initial patterns have large variations. Local equilibrium dynamics under which the size of each cell is flexibly adapted to its local environment are compared with non-local equilibrium dynamics. The local equilibrium dynamics produce more natural cellular patterns.","author":"Nagai, Tatsuzo and Honda, Hisao","journal":"Phil. Mag. B: Phys. Cond. Matt., Stat. Mech., Elec. Opt. Mag. Prop.","pmid":"23206559","title":"{A dynamic cell model for the formation of epithelial tissues}","volume":"81","pages":"699-719","doi":"10.1080/13642810108205772","year":"2001"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Nagano2013","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"7d9e08c3169fedf6","fields":{"title":"Single-cell Hi-C reveals cell-to-cell variability in chromosome structure","author":"Nagano, Takashi and Lubling, Yaniv and Stevens, Tim J and Schoenfelder, Stefan and Yaffe, Eitan and Dean, Wendy and Laue, Ernest D and Tanay, Amos and Fraser, Peter","journal":"Nature","volume":"502","number":"7469","pages":"59--64","year":"2013","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group UK London"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Nava2020","verdict":"unverified","source":"none","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"","fields":{"title":"Heterochromatin-Driven Nuclear Softening Protects the Genome against Mechanical Stress-Induced Damage","journal":"Cell","volume":"181","number":"4","pages":"800-817.e22","year":"2020","issn":"0092-8674","doi":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2020.03.052","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0092867420303457","author":"Michele M. Nava and Yekaterina A. Miroshnikova and Leah C. Biggs and Daniel B. Whitefield and Franziska Metge and Jorge Boucas and Helena Vihinen and Eija Jokitalo and Xinping Li and Juan Manuel {García Arcos} and Bernd Hoffmann and Rudolf Merkel and Carien M. Niessen and Kris Noel Dahl and Sara A. Wickström"},"diffs":[],"failure_note":"not found at the registries — books published before DOIs often are not registered; the entry may still be correct"},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Nnetu2012a","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Collective cell migration is an important feature of wound healing, as well as embryonic and tumor development. The origin of collective cell migration is mainly intercellular interactions through effects such as a line tension preventing cells from detaching from the boundary. In contrast, in this study, we show for the first time that the formation of a constant cell front of a monolayer can also be maintained by the dynamics of the underlying migrating single cells. Ballistic motion enables the maintenance of the integrity of the sheet, while a slowed down dynamics and glass-like behavior cause jamming of cells at the front when two monolayers - even of the same cell type - meet. By employing a velocity autocorrelation function to investigate the cell dynamics in detail, we found a compressed exponential decay as described by the Kohlrausch-William-Watts function of the form , with 1.5 $\\beta$(t) 1.8. This clearly shows that although migrating cells are an active, non-equilibrium system, the cell monolayer behaves in a glass-like way, which requires jamming as a part of intercellular interactions. Since it is the dynamics which determine the integrity of the cell sheet and its front for weakly interacting cells, it becomes evident why changes of the migratory behavior during epithelial to mesenchymal transition can result in the escape of single cells and metastasis. {\\textcopyright} IOP Publishing and Deutsche Physikalische Gesellschaft.","author":"Nnetu, Kenechukwu David and Knorr, Melanie and K{\\\"{a}}s, Josef and Zink, Mareike","doi":"10.1088/1367-2630/14/11/115012","issn":"13672630","journal":"New J. Phys.","title":"{The impact of jamming on boundaries of collectively moving weak-interacting cells}","year":"2012"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Nonaka2013","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"4746f86b5bf824f5","fields":{"title":"Regulation of cerebral cortex size and folding by expansion of basal progenitors","author":"Nonaka-Kinoshita, M. and et. al.","journal":"EMBO J.","volume":"32","pages":"1817-1828","year":"2013"},"diffs":[{"field":"authors","in_document":"Nonaka-Kinoshita, M. and et. al.","in_source":"Miki Nonaka‐Kinoshita and Isabel Reillo and Benedetta Artegiani and Maria Ángeles Martínez‐Martínez and Mark Nelson and Víctor Borrell and Federico Calegari","severity":"high"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Nulty2015","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"299c182252936af7","fields":{"title":"Radial glial cells organize the central nervous system via microtubule dependant processes","author":"Nulty, Jessica and Alsaffar, Mohamed and Barry, Denis","journal":"brain research","volume":"1625","pages":"171--179","year":"2015","publisher":"Elsevier"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Okuda2013","verdict":"partial","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Morphogenesis of tissues in organ development is accompanied by large three-dimensional (3D) deformations, in which mechanical interactions among multiple cells are spatiotemporally regulated. To reveal the deformation mechanisms, in this study, we developed the reversible network reconnection (RNR) model. The model is developed on the basis of 3D vertex model, which expresses a multicellular aggregate as a network composed of vertices. 3D vertex models have successfully simulated morphogenetic dynamics by expressing cellular rearrangements as network reconnections. However, the network reconnections in 3D vertex models can cause geometrical irreversibility, energetic inconsistency, and topological irreversibility, therefore inducing unphysical results and failures in simulating large deformations. To resolve these problems, we introduced (1) a new definition of the shapes of polygonal faces between cellular polyhedrons, (2) an improved condition for network reconnections, (3) a new condition for potential energy functions, and (4) a new constraint condition for the shapes of polygonal faces that represent cell-cell boundaries. Mathematical and computational analyses demonstrated that geometrical irreversibility, energetic inconsistency, and topological irreversibility were resolved by suppressing the geometrical gaps in the network and avoiding the generation of irreversible network patterns in reconnections. Lastly, to demonstrate the applicability of the RNR model, we simulated tissue deformation of growing cell sheets and showed that our model can simulate large tissue deformations, in which large changes occur in the local curvatures and layer formations of tissues. Thus, the RNR model enables in silico recapitulation of complex tissue morphogenesis. {\\textcopyright} 2012 Springer-Verlag.","author":"Okuda, Satoru and Inoue, Yasuhiro and Eiraku, Mototsugu and Sasai, Yoshiki and Adachi, Taiji","doi":"10.1007/s10237-012-0430-7","file":":C\\:/Users/Tao/AppData/Local/Mendeley Ltd./Mendeley Desktop/Downloaded/Okuda et al. - 2013 - Reversible network reconnection model for simulating large deformation in dynamic tissue morphogenesis.pdf:pdf","issn":"16177959","journal":"Biomechanics and Modeling in Mechanobiology","keywords":"Large deformation,Multicellular dynamics,Reversible network reconnection model,Tissue morphogenesis,Vertex model","mendeley-groups":"SJTU/JM Schwarz/BRAIN_ORGANOIDS,SJTU/JM Schwarz/3D Vertex Model,SJTU/JM Schwarz/references","number":"4","pages":"627--644","pmid":"22941051","title":"{Reversible network reconnection model for simulating large deformation in dynamic tissue morphogenesis}","volume":"12","year":"2013"},"diffs":[{"field":"year","in_document":"2013","in_source":"2012","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Okuda2015","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"a43874471742cdea","fields":{"title":"Three-dimensional vertex model for simulating multicellular morphogenesis","author":"Okuda, Satoru and Inoue, Yasuhiro and Adachi, Taiji","journal":"Biophysics and Physicobiology","volume":"12","pages":"13--20","year":"2015","publisher":"The Biophysical Society of Japan"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Okuda2018","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"4dbf1e51650a84c1","fields":{"title":"Combining Turing and 3D vertex models reproduces autonomous multicellular morphogenesis with undulation, tubulation, and branching","author":"Okuda, Satoru and Miura, Takashi and Inoue, Yasuhiro and Adachi, Taiji and Eiraku, Mototsugu","journal":"Scientific Reports","volume":"8","number":"1","pages":"1--15","year":"2018","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Okuda2021","verdict":"unverified","source":"none","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"","fields":{"title":"Polarized interfacial tension induces collective migration of cells, as a cluster, in a three-dimensional tissue","author":"Okuda, Satoru and Sato, Katsuhiko","journal":"arXiv preprint arXiv:2104.13059","year":"2021"},"diffs":[],"failure_note":"not found at the registries — books published before DOIs often are not registered; the entry may still be correct"},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Palsson2008","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"A three-dimensional mathematical model is used to determine the effects of adhesion and cell signalling on cell movements during the aggregation and slug stages of Dictyostelium discoideum (Dd) and to visualize cell sorting. The building blocks of the model are individual deformable ellipsoidal cells, where movement depends on internal parameter state (cell size and stiffness) and on external cues from the neighboring cells, extracellular matrix, and chemical signals. Cell movement and deformation are calculated from equations of motion using the total force acting on each cell, ensuring that forces are balanced. The simulations show that the sorting patterns of prestalk and prespore cells, emerging during the slug stage, depend critically on the type of cell adhesion and not just on chemotactic differences between cells. This occurs because cell size and stiffness can prevent the otherwise faster cells from passing the slower cells. The patterns are distinctively different when the prestalk cells are more or less adhesive than the prespore cells. These simulations suggest that sorting is not solely due to differential chemotaxis, and that differences in both adhesion strength and type between different cell types play a very significant role, both in Dictyostelium and other systems. {\\textcopyright} 2008 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.","author":"Palsson, Eirikur","doi":"10.1016/j.jtbi.2008.05.004","issn":"00225193","journal":"J. Theor. Biol.","keywords":"Deformable cells,Dictyostelium,Differential cell adhesion,Ellipsoidal cells,Pattern formation,Prestalk","title":"{A 3-D model used to explore how cell adhesion and stiffness affect cell sorting and movement in multicellular systems}","year":"2008"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Park2015","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"20a1009d1c803d99","fields":{"title":"Unjamming and cell shape in the asthmatic airway epithelium","author":"Park, Jin-Ah and Kim, Jae Hun and Bi, Dapeng and Mitchel, Jennifer A and Qazvini, Nader Taheri and Tantisira, Kelan and Park, Chan Young and McGill, Maureen and Kim, Sae-Hoon and Gweon, Bomi and others","journal":"Nature Materials","volume":"14","number":"10","pages":"1040--1048","year":"2015","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Parker2020","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"831fc449399d03ed","fields":{"abstract":"Cellularized tissue and polymer networks can both transition from floppy to rigid as a function of their control parameters, and, yet, the two systems often mechanically interact, which may affect their respective rigidities. To study this interaction, we consider a vertex model with interfacial tension (a spheroid) embedded in a spring network in two dimensions. We identify two regimes with different global spheroid shapes, governed by the pressure resulting from competition between interfacial tension and tension in the network. In the first regime, the tissue remains compact, while in the second, a cavitation-like instability leads to the emergence of gaps at the tissue-network interface. Intriguingly, compression of the tissue promotes fluidization, while tension promotes cellular alignment and rigidification, with the mechanisms driving rigidification differing on either side of the instability.","archiveprefix":"arXiv","arxivid":"2006.16203","author":"Parker, Amanda and Marchetti, M. Cristina and Manning, M. Lisa and Schwarz, J. M.","eprint":"2006.16203","file":":C\\:/Users/Tao/AppData/Local/Mendeley Ltd./Mendeley Desktop/Downloaded/Parker et al. - 2020 - How does the extracellular matrix affect the rigidity of an embedded spheroid.pdf:pdf","issn":"23318422","journal":"arXiv","mendeley-groups":"SJTU/JM Schwarz,SJTU/JM Schwarz/3D Vertex Model,SJTU/JM Schwarz/references","number":"1","title":"{How does the extracellular matrix affect the rigidity of an embedded spheroid?}","year":"2020"},"diffs":[{"field":"year","in_document":"2020","in_source":"2025","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Paulose2012","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"417c8395f1c7efd9","fields":{"title":"Fluctuating shells under pressure","author":"Paulose, Jayson and Vliegenthart, Gerard A and Gompper, Gerhard and Nelson, David R","journal":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","volume":"109","number":"48","pages":"19551--19556","year":"2012","publisher":"National Acad Sciences"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Pawlizak2015","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"We analyze the mechanical properties of three epithelial/mesenchymal cell lines (MCF-10A, MDA-MB-231, MDA-MB-436) that exhibit a shift in E-, N- and P-cadherin levels characteristic of an epithelial-mesenchymal transition associated with processes such as metastasis, to quantify the role of cell cohesion in cell sorting and compartmentalization. We develop a unique set of methods to measure cell-cell adhesiveness, cell stiffness and cell shapes, and compare the results to predictions from cell sorting in mixtures of cell populations. We find that the final sorted state is extremely robust among all three cell lines independent of epithelial or mesenchymal state, suggesting that cell sorting may play an important role in organization and boundary formation in tumours. We find that surface densities of adhesive molecules do not correlate with measured cell-cell adhesion, but do correlate with cell shapes, cell stiffness and the rate at which cells sort, in accordance with an extended version of the differential adhesion hypothesis (DAH). Surprisingly, the DAH does not correctly predict the final sorted state. This suggests that these tissues are not behaving as immiscible fluids, and that dynamical effects such as directional motility, friction and jamming may play an important role in tissue compartmentalization across the epithelial-mesenchymal transition.","author":"Pawlizak, Steve and Fritsch, Anatol W. and Grosser, Steffen and Ahrens, Dave and Thalheim, Tobias and Riedel, Stefanie and Kie{\\ss}ling, Tobias R. and Oswald, Linda and Zink, Mareike and Manning, M. Lisa and K{\\\"{a}}s, Josef A.","issn":"13672630","journal":"New J. Phys.","keywords":"atomic force microscopy,cadherins,cell segregation and sorting,cellcell adhesion,compartmentalization,differential adhesion,droplet culture","title":"{Testing the differential adhesion hypothesis across the epithelial-mesenchymal transition}","volume":"17","pages":"091001","year":"2015","doi":"10.1088/1367-2630/17/8/083049"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"PhysRevE.85.031907","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"title":"Kinetic Monte Carlo and cellular particle dynamics simulations of multicellular systems","author":"Flenner, Elijah and Janosi, Lorant and Barz, Bogdan and Neagu, Adrian and Forgacs, Gabor and Kosztin, Ioan","journal":"Phys. Rev. E","volume":"85","issue":"3","pages":"031907","numpages":"10","year":"2012","month":"Mar","publisher":"American Physical Society","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevE.85.031907","url":"https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevE.85.031907"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"PhysRevLett.101.148105","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"title":"Cell Sorting in Three Dimensions: Topology, Fluctuations, and Fluidlike Instabilities","author":"Hutson, M. Shane and Brodland, G. Wayne and Yang, Justina and Viens, Denis","journal":"Phys. Rev. Lett.","volume":"101","issue":"14","pages":"148105","numpages":"4","year":"2008","month":"Oct","publisher":"American Physical Society","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.101.148105","url":"https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevLett.101.148105"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"PhysRevLett.104.218101","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"title":"Aspiration of Biological Viscoelastic Drops","author":"Guevorkian, Karine and Colbert, Marie-Jos\\'ee and Durth, M\\'elanie and Dufour, Sylvie and Brochard-Wyart, Fran\\ifmmode \\mbox{\\c{c}}\\else \\c{c}\\fi{}oise","journal":"Phys. Rev. Lett.","volume":"104","issue":"21","pages":"218101","numpages":"4","year":"2010","month":"May","publisher":"American Physical Society","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.104.218101","url":"https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevLett.104.218101"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Rakic1988","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"7216d9c2d4317030","fields":{"title":"Specification of cerebral cortical areas","author":"Rakic, Pasko","journal":"Science","volume":"241","number":"4862","pages":"170","year":"1988","publisher":"The American Association for the Advancement of Science"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Rakic1995","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"ee10826e8342d082","fields":{"title":"A small step for the cell, a giant leap for mankind: a hypothesis of neocortical expansion during evolution","author":"Rakic, Pasko","journal":"Trends in neurosciences","volume":"18","number":"9","pages":"383--388","year":"1995","publisher":"Elsevier"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Rakic2007","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"fe4fe850770c9ce3","fields":{"title":"The radial edifice of cortical architecture: from neuronal silhouettes to genetic engineering","author":"Rakic, Pasko","journal":"Brain research reviews","volume":"55","number":"2","pages":"204--219","year":"2007","publisher":"Elsevier"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Ramani2017","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"59f4a829eaa331f2","fields":{"title":"Massively multiplex single-cell Hi-C","author":"Ramani, Vijay and Deng, Xinxian and Qiu, Ruolan and Gunderson, Kevin L and Steemers, Frank J and Disteche, Christine M and Noble, William S and Duan, Zhijun and Shendure, Jay","journal":"Nature methods","volume":"14","number":"3","pages":"263--266","year":"2017","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group US New York"},"diffs":[{"field":"authors","in_document":"Ramani, Vijay and Deng, Xinxian and Qiu, Ruolan and Gunderson, Kevin L and Steemers, Frank J and Disteche, Christine M and Noble, William S and Duan, Zhijun and Shendure, Jay","in_source":"Vijay Ramani and Xinxian Deng and Kevin L Gunderson and Frank J Steemers and Christine M Disteche and William S Noble and Zhijun Duan and Jay Shendure","severity":"high"},{"field":"year","in_document":"2017","in_source":"2016","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Ramani2020","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"980abcba1c079b5a","fields":{"title":"SARS-CoV-2 targets neurons of 3D human brain organoids","author":"Ramani, Anand and M{\\\"u}ller, Lisa and Ostermann, Philipp N and Gabriel, Elke and Abida-Islam, Pranty and M{\\\"u}ller-Schiffmann, Andreas and Mariappan, Aruljothi and Goureau, Olivier and Gruell, Henning and Walker, Andreas and others","journal":"The EMBO Journal","volume":"39","number":"20","pages":"e106230","year":"2020"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Rao2014","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"1a98f7805d7c4987","fields":{"title":"A 3D map of the human genome at kilobase resolution reveals principles of chromatin looping","author":"Rao, Suhas SP and Huntley, Miriam H and Durand, Neva C and Stamenova, Elena K and Bochkov, Ivan D and Robinson, James T and Sanborn, Adrian L and Machol, Ido and Omer, Arina D and Lander, Eric S and others","journal":"Cell","volume":"159","number":"7","pages":"1665--1680","year":"2014","publisher":"Elsevier"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Reiner1993","verdict":"unverified","source":"none","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"","fields":{"title":"Isolation of a Miller-Dieker lissenchephaly gene containing G protein beta-subunit-like repeats","author":"Reiner, O. and et. al.","journal":"Nature","volume":"364","pages":"717-721","year":"1993"},"diffs":[],"failure_note":"not found at the registries — books published before DOIs often are not registered; the entry may still be correct"},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Richman1975","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"95c4d668062dfa79","fields":{"title":"Mechanical model of brain convolutional development","author":"Richman, David P and Stewart, R Malcolm and Hutchinson, John W and Caviness, Verne S","journal":"Science","volume":"189","number":"4196","pages":"18--21","year":"1975","publisher":"JSTOR"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Rozman2020","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"5363be7465badc37","fields":{"title":"Collective cell mechanics of epithelial shells with organoid-like morphologies","author":"Rozman, Jan and Krajnc, Matej and Ziherl, Primo{\\v{z}}","journal":"Nature Communications","volume":"11","number":"1","pages":"1--9","year":"2020","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Rubsam2017","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0bd61399124776ad","fields":{"abstract":"Generation of a barrier in multi-layered epithelia like the epidermis requires restricted positioning of functional tight junctions (TJ) to the most suprabasal viable layer. This positioning necessitates tissue-level polarization of junctions and the cytoskeleton through unknown mechanisms. Using quantitative whole-mount imaging, genetic ablation, and traction force microscopy and atomic force microscopy, we find that ubiquitously localized E-cadherin coordinates tissue polarization of tension-bearing adherens junction (AJ) and F-actin organization to allow formation of an apical TJ network only in the uppermost viable layer. Molecularly, E-cadherin localizes and tunes EGFR activity and junctional tension to inhibit premature TJ complex formation in lower layers while promoting increased tension and TJ stability in the granular layer 2. In conclusion, our data identify an E-cadherin-dependent mechanical circuit that integrates adhesion, contractile forces and biochemical signaling to drive the polarized organization of junctional tension necessary to build an in vivo epithelial barrier.","author":"R{\\\"{u}}bsam, Matthias and Mertz, Aaron F. and Kubo, Akiharu and Marg, Susanna and J{\\\"{u}}ngst, Christian and Goranci-Buzhala, Gladiola and Schauss, Astrid C. and Horsley, Valerie and Dufresne, Eric R. and Moser, Markus and Ziegler, Wolfgang and Amagai, Masayuki and Wickstr{\\\"{o}}m, Sara A. and Niessen, Carien M.","doi":"10.1038/s41467-017-01170-7","issn":"20411723","journal":"Nat. Commun.","title":"{E-cadherin integrates mechanotransduction and EGFR signaling to control junctional tissue polarization and tight junction positioning}","year":"2017"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Runser2024","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"3832a2c5916a034f","fields":{"title":"SimuCell3D: three-dimensional simulation of tissue mechanics with cell polarization","author":"Runser, Steve and Vetter, Roman and Iber, Dagmar","journal":"Nature Computational Science","pages":"1--11","year":"2024","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group US New York"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Rycroft2009","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"author":"Rycroft, Chris H.","doi":"10.1063/1.3215722","file":":C\\:/Users/Tao/Dropbox/SJTU/VertexModel/references/946741.pdf:pdf","issn":"10541500","journal":"Chaos","mendeley-groups":"SJTU/JM Schwarz/references","pages":"041111","pmid":"20059195","title":"{VORO++: A three-dimensional Voronoi cell library in C++}","volume":"19","year":"2009"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"STEINBERG2007281","verdict":"unverified","source":"none","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"","fields":{"title":"Differential adhesion in morphogenesis: a modern view","journal":"Current Opinion in Genetics & Development","volume":"17","number":"4","pages":"281 - 286","year":"2007","note":"Pattern formation and developmental mechanisms","issn":"0959-437X","doi":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gde.2007.05.002","url":"http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959437X07001062","author":"Malcolm S Steinberg","abstract":"The spreading of one embryonic tissue over another, the sorting out of their cells when intermixed and the formation of intertissue boundaries respected by the motile border cells all have counterparts in the behavior of immiscible liquids. The ‘differential adhesion hypothesis’ (DAH) explains these liquid-like tissue behaviors as consequences of the generation of tissue surface and interfacial tensions arising from the adhesion energies between motile cells. The experimental verification of the DAH, the recent computational models simulating adhesion-mediated morphogenesis, and the evidence concerning the role of differential adhesion in a number of morphodynamic events, including teleost epiboly, the specification of boundaries between rhombomeres in the developing vertebrate hindbrain, epithelial–mesenchymal transitions in embryos, and malignant invasion are reviewed here."},"diffs":[],"failure_note":"not found at the registries — books published before DOIs often are not registered; the entry may still be correct"},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Sahu2020a","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"abstract":"Surface tension governed by differential adhesion can drive fluid particle mixtures to sort into separate regions, i.e., demix. Does the same phenomenon occur in confluent biological tissues? We begin to answer this question for epithelial monolayers with a combination of theory via a vertex model and experiments on keratinocyte monolayers. Vertex models are distinct from particle models in that the interactions between the cells are shape-based, as opposed to distance-dependent. We investigate whether a disparity in cell shape or size alone is sufficient to drive demixing in bidisperse vertex model fluid mixtures. Surprisingly, we observe that both types of bidisperse systems robustly mix on large lengthscales. On the other hand, shape disparity generates slight demixing over a few cell diameters, a phenomenon we term micro-demixing. This result can be understood by examining the differential energy barriers for neighbor exchanges (T1 transitions). Experiments with mixtures of wild-type and E-cadherin-deficient keratinocytes on a substrate are consistent with the predicted phenomenon of micro-demixing, which biology may exploit to create subtle patterning. The robustness of mixing at large scales, however, suggests that despite some differences in cell shape and size, progenitor cells can readily mix throughout a developing tissue until acquiring means of recognizing cells of different types.","archiveprefix":"arXiv","arxivid":"1905.00657","author":"Sahu, Preeti and Sussman, Daniel M. and R{\\\"{u}}bsam, Matthias and Mertz, Aaron F. and Horsley, Valerie and Dufresne, Eric R. and Niessen, Carien M. and Marchetti, M. Cristina and Manning, M. Lisa and Schwarz, J. M.","doi":"10.1039/c9sm01084j","eprint":"1905.00657","issn":"17446848","journal":"Soft Matter","pmid":"32196025","title":"{Small-scale demixing in confluent biological tissues}","year":"2020"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Sahu2020b","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"abstract":"The fluidity of biological tissues-whether cells can change neighbors and rearrange-is important for their function. In traditional materials, researchers have used linear response functions, such as the shear modulus, to accurately predict whether a material will behave as a fluid. Similarly, in disordered 2D vertex models for confluent biological tissues, the shear modulus becomes zero precisely when the cells can change neighbors and the tissue fluidizes, at a critical value of control parameter s0∗ = 3.81. However, the ordered ground states of 2D vertex models become linearly unstable at a lower value of control parameter (3.72), suggesting that there may be a decoupling between linear and nonlinear response. We demonstrate that the linear response does not correctly predict the nonlinear behavior in these systems: when the control parameter is between 3.72 and 3.81, cells cannot freely change neighbors even though the shear modulus is zero. These results highlight that the linear response of vertex models should not be expected to generically predict their rheology. We develop a simple geometric ansatz that correctly predicts the nonlinear response, which may serve as a framework for making nonlinear predictions in other vertex-like models.","author":"Sahu, Preeti and Kang, Janice and Erdemci-Tandogan, Gonca and Manning, M. Lisa","doi":"10.1039/c9sm01068h","issn":"17446848","journal":"Soft Matter","title":"{Linear and nonlinear mechanical responses can be quite different in models for biological tissues}","year":"2020"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Sahu2021","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"42f02834ebc3846c","fields":{"title":"Geometric signatures of tissue surface tension in a three-dimensional model of confluent tissue","author":"Sahu, Preeti and Schwarz, J. M. and Manning, M. Lisa","journal":"New Journal of Physics","volume":"23","number":"9","pages":"093043","year":"2021","publisher":"IOP Publishing"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Sanders2022","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"363c4ef6bcdd84f9","fields":{"title":"Loops, topologically associating domains, compartments, and territories are elastic and robust to dramatic nuclear volume swelling","author":"Sanders, Jacob T and Golloshi, Rosela and Das, Priyojit and Xu, Yang and Terry, Peyton H and Nash, Darrian G and Dekker, Job and McCord, Rachel Patton","journal":"Scientific Reports","volume":"12","number":"1","pages":"1--19","year":"2022","publisher":"Springer"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Sato2009","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"f162e8e0702f52a8","fields":{"title":"Single Lgr5 stem cells build crypt-villus structures in vitro without a mesenchymal niche","author":"Sato, Toshiro and Vries, Robert G and Snippert, Hugo J and Van De Wetering, Marc and Barker, Nick and Stange, Daniel E and Van Es, Johan H and Abo, Arie and Kujala, Pekka and Peters, Peter J and others","journal":"Nature","volume":"459","number":"7244","pages":"262--265","year":"2009","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group"},"diffs":[{"field":"authors","in_document":"Sato, Toshiro and Vries, Robert G and Snippert, Hugo J and Van De Wetering, Marc and Barker, Nick and Stange, Daniel E and Van Es, Johan H and Abo, Arie and Kujala, Pekka and Peters, Peter J and others","in_source":"Toshiro Sato and Robert G. Vries and Hugo J. Snippert and Marc van de Wetering and Nick Barker and Daniel E. Stange and Johan H. van Es and Arie Abo and Pekka Kujala and Peter J. Peters and Hans Clevers","severity":"high"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Sharp2019","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"abstract":"Although cell shape can reflect the mechanical and biochemical properties of the cell and its environment, quantification of 3D cell shapes within 3D tissues remains difficult, typically requiring digital reconstruction from a stack of 2D images. We investigate a simple alternative technique to extract information about the 3D shapes of cells in a tissue; this technique connects the ensemble of 3D shapes in the tissue with the distribution of 2D shapes observed in independent 2D slices. Using cell vertex model geometries, we find that the distribution of 2D shapes allows clear determination of the mean value of a 3D shape index. We analyze the errors that may arise in practice in the estimation of the mean 3D shape index from 2D imagery and find that typically only a few dozen cells in 2D imagery are required to reduce uncertainty below 2{\\%}. Even though we developed the method for isotropic animal tissues, we demonstrate it on an anisotropic plant tissue. This framework could also be naturally extended to estimate additional 3D geometric features and quantify their uncertainty in other materials.","author":"Sharp, Tristan A. and Merkel, Matthias and {Lisa Manning}, M. and Liu, Andrea J.","doi":"10.1371/journal.pone.0209892","issn":"19326203","journal":"PLoS One","title":"{Inferring statistical properties of 3D cell geometry from 2D slices}","year":"2019"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Shi2018","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"4e9375bbaaf04ce1","fields":{"title":"Interphase human chromosome exhibits out of equilibrium glassy dynamics","author":"Shi, Guang and Liu, Lei and Hyeon, Changbong and Thirumalai, Dave","journal":"Nature communications","volume":"9","number":"1","pages":"3161","year":"2018","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group UK London"},"diffs":[{"field":"year","in_document":"2018","in_source":"2017","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Shi2021","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"dfe468daad047376","fields":{"title":"From Hi-C contact map to three-dimensional organization of interphase human chromosomes","author":"Shi, Guang and Thirumalai, D","journal":"Physical Review X","volume":"11","number":"1","pages":"011051","year":"2021","publisher":"APS"},"diffs":[{"field":"year","in_document":"2021","in_source":"2020","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Shi2023","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"07a13a1024705e33","fields":{"title":"A maximum-entropy model to predict 3D structural ensembles of chromatin from pairwise distances with applications to interphase chromosomes and structural variants","author":"Shi, Guang and Thirumalai, D","journal":"Nature Communications","volume":"14","number":"1","pages":"1150","year":"2023","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group UK London"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Song2016","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"c7922e60009e4157","fields":{"abstract":"Cells with different cohesive properties self-assemble in a spatiotemporal and context-dependent manner. Previous studies on cell self-organization mainly focused on the spontaneous structural development within a short period of time during which the cell numbers remained constant. However the effect of cell proliferation over time on the self-organization of cells is largely unexplored. Here, we studied the spatiotemporal dynamics of self-organization of a co-culture of MDA-MB-231 and MCF10A cells seeded in a well defined space (i.e. non-adherent microfabricated wells). When cell-growth was chemically inhibited, high cohesive MCF10A cells formed a core surrounded by low cohesive MDA-MB-231 cells on the periphery, consistent with the differential adhesion hypothesis (DAH). Interestingly, this aggregate morphology was completely inverted when the cells were free to grow. At an initial seeding ratio of 1:1 (MDA-MB-231:MCF10A), the fast growing MCF10A cells segregated in the periphery while the slow growing MDA-MB-231 cells stayed in the core. Another morphology developed at an inequal seeding ratio (4:1), that is, the cell mixtures developed a side-by-side aggregate morphology. We conclude that the cell self-organization depends not only on the cell cohesive properties but also on the cell seeding ratio and proliferation. Furthermore, by taking advantage of the cell self-organization, we purified human embryonic stem cells-derived pancreatic progenitors (hESCs-PPs) from co-cultured feeder cells without using any additional tools or labels.","author":"Song, Wei and Tung, Chih Kuan and Lu, Yen Chun and Pardo, Yehudah and Wu, Mingming and Das, Moumita and Kao, Der I. and Chen, Shuibing and Ma, Minglin","issn":"17446848","journal":"Soft Matt.","title":"{Dynamic self-organization of microwell-aggregated cellular mixtures}","volume":"12","pages":"5739","year":"2016"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Srivatsa2015","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"e8edc090b5657c3c","fields":{"title":"Sip1 downstream Effector ninein controls neocortical axonal growth, ipsilateral branching, and microtubule growth and stability","author":"Srivatsa, Swathi and Parthasarathy, Srinivas and Moln{\\'a}r, Zolt{\\'a}n and Tarabykin, Victor","journal":"Neuron","volume":"85","number":"5","pages":"998--1012","year":"2015","publisher":"Elsevier"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Staddon2019","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"82626f357a99d40b","fields":{"title":"Mechanosensitive junction remodeling promotes robust epithelial morphogenesis","author":"Staddon, Michael F. and Cavanaugh, Kate E. and Munro, Edwin M. and Gardel, Margaret L. and Banerjee, Shiladitya","journal":"Biophysical Journal","volume":"117","number":"9","pages":"1739--1750","year":"2019","publisher":"Elsevier"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Stahl2013","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"2c25ed07985508c1","fields":{"title":"Trnp1 regulates expansion and folding of the mammalian cerebral cortex by control of radial glial fate","author":"Stahl, R. and et. al.","journal":"Cell","volume":"153","pages":"535-549","year":"2013"},"diffs":[{"field":"authors","in_document":"Stahl, R. and et. al.","in_source":"Ronny Stahl and Tessa Walcher and Camino De Juan Romero and Gregor Alexander Pilz and Silvia Cappello and Martin Irmler and José Miguel Sanz-Aquela and Johannes Beckers and Robert Blum and Víctor Borrell and Magdalena Götz","severity":"high"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Staple2010a","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"abstract":"Epithelia are sheets of cells that are dynamically remodelled by cell division and cell death during development. Here we describe the cell shapes and packings as networks of polygons: stable and stationary network configurations obey force balance and are represented as local minima of a potential function. We characterize the physical properties of this vertex model, including the set of ground states, and the energetics of topological rearrangements. We furthermore discuss a quasistatic description of cell division that allows us to study the mechanics and dynamics of tissue remodelling during growth. The biophysics of cells and their rearrangements can account for the morphology of cell packings observed in experiments.","author":"Staple, D. B. and Farhadifar, R. and R{\\\"{o}}per, J. C. and Aigouy, B. and Eaton, S. and J{\\\"{u}}licher, F.","doi":"10.1140/epje/i2010-10677-0","journal":"Eur. Phys. J. E","pmid":"21082210","volume":"33","pages":"117-127","title":"{Mechanics and remodelling of cell packings in epithelia}","year":"2010"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Stegmaier2016","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"abstract":"We present the Real-time Accurate Cell-shape Extractor (RACE), a high-throughput image analysis framework for automated three-dimensional cell segmentation in large-scale images. RACE is 55-330 times faster and 2-5 times more accurate than state-of-the-art methods. We demonstrate the generality of RACE by extracting cell-shape information from entire Drosophila, zebrafish, and mouse embryos imaged with confocal and light-sheet microscopes. Using RACE, we automatically reconstructed cellular-resolution tissue anisotropy maps across developing Drosophila embryos and quantified differences in cell-shape dynamics in wild-type and mutant embryos. We furthermore integrated RACE with our framework for automated cell lineaging and performed joint segmentation and cell tracking in entire Drosophila embryos. RACE processed these terabyte-sized datasets on a single computer within 1.4 days. RACE is easy to use, as it requires adjustment of only three parameters, takes full advantage of state-of-the-art multi-core processors and graphics cards, and is available as open-source software for Windows, Linux, and Mac OS. Stegmaier et al. present the open-source software RACE for automated three-dimensional cell segmentation in large-scale microscopy images. RACE rapidly reconstructs cell shapes in entire developing Drosophila, zebrafish, and mouse embryos imaged with confocal or light-sheet microscopy and quantifies cell-shape dynamics and phenotypic differences in wild-type and mutant embryos.","author":"Stegmaier, Johannes and Amat, Fernando and Lemon, William C. and McDole, Katie and Wan, Yinan and Teodoro, George and Mikut, Ralf and Keller, Philipp J.","doi":"10.1016/j.devcel.2015.12.028","issn":"18781551","journal":"Dev. Cell","title":"{Real-Time Three-Dimensional Cell Segmentation in Large-Scale Microscopy Data of Developing Embryos}","year":"2016"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Steinberg1963a","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"abstract":"Some morphogenetic tissue movements and the sorting out of embryonic cells may have a common explanation.","author":"Steinberg, Malcolm S.","doi":"10.1126/science.141.3579.401","issn":"00368075","journal":"Sci. Sci.","title":"{Reconstruction of tissues by dissociated cells}","year":"1963"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Stephens2017","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"33cae7c6589870d3","fields":{"title":"Chromatin and lamin A determine two different mechanical response regimes of the cell nucleus","author":"Stephens, Andrew D and Banigan, Edward J and Adam, Stephen A and Goldman, Robert D and Marko, John F","journal":"Molecular biology of the cell","volume":"28","number":"14","pages":"1984--1996","year":"2017","publisher":"Am Soc Cell Biol"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Streichan2018","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"abstract":"During embryogenesis tissue layers undergo morphogenetic flow rearranging and folding into specific shapes. While developmental biology has identified key genes and local cellular processes, global coordination of tissue remodeling at the organ scale remains unclear. Here, we combine in toto light-sheet microscopy of the Drosophila embryo with quantitative analysis and physical modeling to relate cellular flow with the patterns of force generation during the gastrulation process. We find that the complex spatio-temporal flow pattern can be predicted from the measured meso-scale myosin density and anisotropy using a simple, effective viscous model of the tissue, achieving close to 90{\\%} accuracy with one time dependent and two constant parameters. Our analysis uncovers the importance of a) spatial modulation of myosin distribution on the scale of the embryo and b) the non-locality of its effect due to mechanical interaction of cells, demonstrating the need for the global perspective in the study of morphogenetic flow.","author":"Streichan, Sebastian J and Lefebvre, Matthew F and Noll, Nicholas and Wieschaus, Eric F and Shraiman, Boris I","doi":"10.7554/elife.27454","journal":"Elife","title":"{Global morphogenetic flow is accurately predicted by the spatial distribution of myosin motors}","year":"2018"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Strom2021","verdict":"unverified","source":"none","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"","fields":{"title":"HP1$\\alpha$ is a chromatin crosslinker that controls nuclear and mitotic chromosome mechanics","author":"Strom, Amy R and Biggs, Ronald J and Banigan, Edward J and Wang, Xiaotao and Chiu, Katherine and Herman, Cameron and Collado, Jimena and Yue, Feng and Ritland Politz, Joan C and Tait, Leah J and others","journal":"Elife","volume":"10","pages":"e63972","year":"2021","publisher":"eLife Sciences Publications, Ltd"},"diffs":[],"failure_note":"not found at the registries — books published before DOIs often are not registered; the entry may still be correct"},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Sudarov2007","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"ca80615b80930651","fields":{"title":"Cerebellum morphogenesis: the foliation pattern is orchestrated by multi-cellular anchoring centers","author":"Sudarov, Anamaria and Joyner, Alexandra L","journal":"Neural Development","volume":"2","number":"1","pages":"26","year":"2007","publisher":"Springer"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Sugimura2016","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"abstract":"Development, homeostasis and regeneration of tissues result from a complex combination of genetics and mechanics, and progresses in the former have been quicker than in the latter. Measurements of in situ forces and stresses appear to be increasingly important to delineate the role of mechanics in development. We review here several emerging techniques: contact manipulation, manipulation using light, visual sensors, and non-mechanical observation techniques. We compare their fields of applications, their advantages and limitations, and their validations. These techniques complement measurements of deformations and of mechanical properties. We argue that such approaches could have a significant impact on our understanding of the development of living tissues in the near future.","author":"Sugimura, Kaoru and Lenne, Pierre Fran{\\c{c}}ois and Graner, Fran{\\c{c}}ois","doi":"10.1242/dev.119776","issn":"14779129","journal":"Dev.","keywords":"Mechanics,Morphogenesis,Quantitative biology","pmid":"26786209","title":"{Measuring forces and stresses in situ in living tissues}","year":"2016"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Sun2013","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"abstract":"We employ a three-dimensional (3D) lattice model based on the kinetic Monte Carlo (KMC) method to study cell self-assembly and cellular aggregate fusion of multicellular aggregate systems. This model is developed to describe and predict time evolution of postprinting morphological structure formation during morphogenesis of tissues or organs in a novel biofabrication process known as bioprinting. In this new technology, live multicellular aggregates are used as the fundamental building blocks, known as the bio-ink, to make tissue or organ constructs via the layer-by-layer deposition technique in biocompatible hydrogels; the printed bio-constructs embedded in the hydrogels are then placed in bioreactors to undergo the fusion process to form the desired functional tissue or organ products. In this paper, we implement the lattice model in an efficient list-based KMC algorithm to simulate the making of a set of tissue/organ constructs in several designer's geometries (a ring, a sheet and a tube), which involves a large number of cells of a single type and various other extracellular matrix materials like agarose etc. We then study the process of cell sorting in fusion of cellular aggregates involving multiple cell types with diverse adhesivities and compatible extracellular hydrogels. The KMC simulations reported in this paper agree qualitatively with the available experimental results. In the meantime, they are also comparable to the results obtained from cellular particle dynamics (CPD) simulations. {\\textcopyright} 2013 The Royal Society of Chemistry.","author":"Sun, Yi and Wang, Qi","doi":"10.1039/c2sm27090k","issn":"17446848","journal":"Soft Matter","title":"{Modeling and simulations of multicellular aggregate self-assembly in biofabrication using kinetic Monte Carlo methods}","year":"2013"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Sussman2017","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"b6a941af8b75861e","fields":{"title":"cellGPU: Massively parallel simulations of dynamic vertex models","author":"Sussman, Daniel M.","journal":"Computer Physics Communications","volume":"219","pages":"400--406","year":"2017","publisher":"Elsevier"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Sussman2018b","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"abstract":"How can dense biological tissue maintain sharp boundaries between coexisting cell populations? We explore this question within a simple vertex model for cells, focusing on the role of topology and tissue surface tension. We show that the ability of cells to independently regulate adhesivity and tension, together with neighbor-based interaction rules, lets them support strikingly unusual interfaces. In particular, we show that mechanical- and fluctuation-based measurements of the effective surface tension of a cellular aggregate yield different results, leading to mechanically soft interfaces that are nevertheless extremely sharp.","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.120.058001","author":"Sussman, Daniel M. and Schwarz, J. M. and Marchetti, M. Cristina and Manning, M. Lisa","volume":"120","pages":"058001","journal":"Phys. Rev. Lett.","title":"{Soft yet Sharp Interfaces in a Vertex Model of Confluent Tissue}","year":"2018"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Tallinen2016","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"0ed3ae67359749aa","fields":{"title":"On the growth and form of cortical convolutions","author":"Tallinen, Tuomas and Chung, Jun Young and Rousseau, Fran{\\c{c}}ois and Girard, Nadine and Lef{\\`e}vre, Julien and Mahadevan, Lakshminarayanan","journal":"Nature Physics","volume":"12","number":"6","pages":"588--593","year":"2016","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Teleman2007","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"abstract":"A fundamental and unresolved problem in animal development is the question of how a growing tissue knows when it has achieved its correct final size. A widely held view suggests that this process is controlled by morphogen gradients, which adapt to tissue size and become flatter as tissue grows, leading eventually to growth arrest. Here, we present evidence that the decapentaplegic (Dpp) morphogen distribution in the developing Drosophila wing imaginal disk does not adapt to disk size. We measure the distribution of a functional Dpp-GFP transgene and the Dpp signal transduced by phospho-Mad and show that the characteristic length scale of the Dpp profile remains approximately constant during growth. This finding suggests an alternative scenario of size determination, where disk size is determined relative to the fixed morphogen distribution by a certain threshold level of morphogen required for growth. We propose that when disk boundary reaches the threshold the arrest of cell proliferation throughout the disk is induced by mechanical stress in the tissue. Mechanical stress is expected to arise from the nonuniformity of morphogen distribution that drives growth. This stress, through a negative feedback on growth, can compensate for the nonuniformity of morphogen, achieving uniform growth with the rate that vanishes when disk boundary reaches the threshold. The mechanism is demonstrated through computer simulations of a tissue growth model that identifies the key assumptions and testable predictions. This analysis provides an alternative hypothesis for the size determination process. Novel experimental approaches will be needed to test this model.","author":"Teleman, A. A. and Hufnagel, L. and Rouault, H. and Shraiman, B. I. and Cohen, S. M.","doi":"10.1073/pnas.0607134104","isbn":"0780386620","issn":"0027-8424","journal":"Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci.","pmid":"17360439","title":"{On the mechanism of wing size determination in fly development}","year":"2007"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Trinkaus1955a","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"abstract":"IMAGES:","author":"Trinkaus, J. P. and Groves, P. W.","doi":"10.1073/pnas.41.10.787","issn":"0027-8424","journal":"Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci.","pmid":"16589751","title":"{DIFFERENTIATION IN CULTURE OF MIXED AGGREGATES OF DISSOCIATED TISSUE CELLS}","year":"1955"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Trujillo2019","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"28ca6e0290867f59","fields":{"author":"Trujillo, C. A. and Gao, R. and Negraes, P. D. and et al.","title":"Complex oscillatory waves emerging from cortical organoids model early human brain network development","publisher":"","year":"2019","journal":"Cell Stem Cell","volume":"25","number":"4","pages":"558-569"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Tsai2019","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"abstract":"An outstanding question in embryo development is how spatial patterns are formed robustly. In the zebrafish spinal cord, neural progenitors form stereotypic stripe-like patterns despite noisy morphogen signaling and large-scale cellular rearrangement required for tissue growth and morphogenesis. We set out to understand the mechanisms underlying this patterning robustness. Our adhesion assays revealed a preference for three neural progenitor types to stabilize contacts with cells of the same type. Genetic analysis uncovered a three-molecule adhesion code, composed of N-cadherin, Cadherin 11, and Protocadherin 19, with unique gene expression profiles for each cell type. Perturbation of the adhesion code results in loss of homotypic preference ex vivo and patterning errors in vivo . Both the cell fate and adhesion code are co-regulated by the common upstream morphogen signal Shh. We propose that robust patterning in tissues undergoing morphogenesis results from a previously unappreciated interplay between morphogen gradient-based patterning and adhesion-based self-organization.","author":"Tsai, Tony Y.-C. and Sikora, Mateusz and Xia, Peng and Colak-Champollion, Tugba and Knaut, Holger and Heisenberg, Carl-Philipp and Megason, Sean G.","doi":"10.1101/803635","journal":"bioRxiv","title":"{An adhesion code ensures robust pattern formation during tissue morphogenesis}","year":"2019"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Turing1990","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"abstract":"It is suggested that a system of chemical substances, called morphogens, reacting together and diffusing through a tissue, is adequate to account for the main phenomena of morphogenesis. Such a system, although it may originally be quite homogeneous, may later develop a pattern or structure due to an instability of the homogeneous equilibrium, which is triggered off by random disturbances. Such reaction-diffusion systems are considered in some detail in the case of an isolated ring of cells, a mathematically convenient, though biologically unusual system. The investigation is chiefly concerned with the onset of instability. It is found that there are six essentially different forms which this may take. In the most interesting form stationary waves appear on the ring. It is suggested that this might account, for instance, for the tentacle patterns on Hydra and for whorled leaves. A system of reactions and diffusion on a sphere is also considered. Such a system appears to account for gastrulation. Another reaction system in two dimensions gives rise to patterns reminiscent of dappling. It is also suggested that stationary waves in two dimensions could account for the phenomena of phyllotaxis. The purpose of this paper is to discuss a possible mechanism by which the genes of a zygote may determine the anatomical structure of the resulting organism. The theory does not make any new hypotheses; it merely suggests that certain well-known physical laws are sufficient to account for many of the facts. The full understanding of the paper requires a good knowledge of mathematics, some biology, and some elementary chemistry. Since readers cannot be expected to be experts in all of these subjects, a number of elementary facts are explained, which can be found in text-books, but whose omission would make the paper difficult reading. {\\textcopyright} 1990 Society for Mathematical Biology.","author":"Turing, A. M.","doi":"10.1007/BF02459572","issn":"00928240","journal":"Bull. Math. Biol.","title":"{The chemical basis of morphogenesis}","year":"1990"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Uhler2017","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"9fabc360e1b2dd79","fields":{"title":"Regulation of genome organization and gene expression by nuclear mechanotransduction","author":"Uhler, Caroline and Shivashankar, GV","journal":"Nature reviews Molecular cell biology","volume":"18","number":"12","pages":"717--727","year":"2017","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group UK London"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Unbekandt2010","verdict":"partial","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"abstract":"Here we describe a novel method in which embryonic kidneys are dissociated into single-cell suspensions and then reaggregated to form organotypic renal structures. Kidney cell reaggregates were transiently cultured with small-molecule Rho kinase inhibitors, which caused ureteric bud structures to form and induced formation of nephrons. These structures displayed normal morphology, expressed appropriate differentiation markers, and were connected at their distal ends to the ureteric buds, thus forming artificial tissues very similar to those found in normal embryonic kidneys. Using this culture method, it was straightforward to make fine-grained chimeras by mixing different cell types or by mixing cells transfected with different constructs before reaggregation. Chimeric renal cultures were formed using mixtures of unmarked normal host embryonic kidney cells and CellTracker-marked WT1 siRNA-carrying cells to test the hypothesis that WT1 is important to a cell's ability to contribute to nephron formation. We found a significant reduction in the ability of WT1 knockdown cells to contribute to nephron formation. This dissociation and reaggregation procedure can also be applied to embryonic lungs and to form coarse-grained hybrid tissues from mixtures of lung and kidney cells. Overall, our protocol allows very simple mixing of cells from different sources or cells subjected to different pretreatments to make fine-grained, highly dispersed chimera tissues. {\\textcopyright} 2010 International Society of Nephrology.","author":"Unbekandt, Mathieu and Davies, Jamie A.","doi":"10.1038/ki.2009.482","issn":"00852538","journal":"Kidney Int.","keywords":"Cell survival,Kidney development,Renal cell biology,Renal development,Tubular epithelium","title":"{Dissociation of embryonic kidneys followed by reaggregation allows the formation of renal tissues}","year":"2010"},"diffs":[{"field":"year","in_document":"2010","in_source":"2009","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"VanEssen1997","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"b8f4e1ef88b6b625","fields":{"title":"A tension-based theory of morphogenesis and compact wiring in the central nervous system","author":"Van Essen, David C","journal":"Nature","volume":"385","number":"6614","pages":"313--318","year":"1997","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"VanOosten2016","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"9a04a941822e2cd8","fields":{"title":"Uncoupling shear and uniaxial elastic moduli of semiflexible biopolymer networks: compression-softening and stretch-stiffening","author":"Van Oosten, Anne S. G. and Vahabi, M. and Licup, A. J. and Sharma, A. and Galie, P. A. and MacKintosh, F. C. and Janmey, P. A.","journal":"Sci. Reps.","volume":"6","pages":"19270","year":"2016","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Vandewalle2005","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"1aec40aac0a15ca8","fields":{"title":"SIP1/ZEB2 induces EMT by repressing genes of different epithelial cell--cell junctions","author":"Vandewalle, Cindy and Comijn, Joke and De Craene, Bram and Vermassen, Petra and Bruyneel, Erik and Andersen, Henriette and Tulchinsky, Eugene and Van Roy, Frans and Berx, Geert","journal":"Nucleic acids research","volume":"33","number":"20","pages":"6566--6578","year":"2005","publisher":"Oxford University Press"},"diffs":[{"field":"authors","in_document":"Vandewalle, Cindy and Comijn, Joke and De Craene, Bram and Vermassen, Petra and Bruyneel, Erik and Andersen, Henriette and Tulchinsky, Eugene and Van Roy, Frans and Berx, Geert","in_source":"C. Vandewalle","severity":"high"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Velasco2019","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"eed645e00e5c6d76","fields":{"title":"Individual brain organoids reproducibly form cell diversity of the human cerebral cortex","author":"Velasco, S. and Kedaigle, A. J. and Simmons, S. K. and Nash, A. and Rocha, M. and Quadrato, G. and Paulsen, B. and Nguyen, L. and Adiconis, X. and Regev, A. and Levin, J. Z. and Arlotta, P.","journal":"Nature","volume":"570","number":"7762","pages":"523-527","year":"2019","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Versaevel2012","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"1f2d9cea57d02efb","fields":{"title":"Spatial coordination between cell and nuclear shape within micropatterned endothelial cells","author":"Versaevel, Marie and Grevesse, Thomas and Gabriele, Sylvain","journal":"Nature communications","volume":"3","number":"1","pages":"671","year":"2012","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group UK London"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Waites2017b","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"abstract":"Exactly a century ago, D'Arcy Thompson set an agenda for understanding tissue development in terms of underlying biophysical, mathematically-tractable mechanisms. One such mechanism, discovered by Steinberg in the 1960s, is adhesion-mediated sorting of cell mixtures into homotypic groups. Interest in this phase separation mechanism has recently surged, partly because of its use to create synthetic biological patterning mechanisms and partly because it has been found to drive events critical to the formation of organoids from stem cells, making the process relevant to biotechnology as well as to basic development. Here, we construct quantitative model of patterning by phase separation, informed by laboratory data, and use it to explore the relationship between degree of adhesive difference and speed, type and extent of resultant patterning. Our results can be used three ways; to predict the outcome of mixing cells with known properties, to estimate the properties required to make some designed organoid system, or to estimate underlying cellular properties from observed behaviour.","author":"Waites, William and Cavaliere, Matteo and Cachat, Elise and Danos, Vincent and Davies, Jamie A.","doi":"10.1101/136366","journal":"bioRxiv","title":"{Organoid And Tissue Patterning Through Phase Separation: Use Of A Vertex Model To Relate Dynamics Of Patterning To Underlying Biophysical Parameters}","year":"2017"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Wang2016","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"630094d56a85fc32","fields":{"title":"Spatial organization of chromatin domains and compartments in single chromosomes","author":"Wang, Siyuan and Su, Jun-Han and Beliveau, Brian J and Bintu, Bogdan and Moffitt, Jeffrey R and Wu, Chao-ting and Zhuang, Xiaowei","journal":"Science","volume":"353","number":"6299","pages":"598--602","year":"2016","publisher":"American Association for the Advancement of Science"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Wang2020","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"85f38a5dcbb8bfac","fields":{"title":"Anisotropy links cell shapes to tissue flow during convergent extension","author":"Wang, Xun and Merkel, Matthias and Sutter, Leo B and Erdemci-Tandogan, Gonca and Manning, M. Lisa and Kasza, Karen E.","journal":"Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences","volume":"117","number":"24","pages":"13541--13551","year":"2020","publisher":"National Acad Sciences"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Warmt2021","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"c6bc7fd7223e6a53","fields":{"title":"Differences in cortical contractile properties between healthy epithelial and cancerous mesenchymal breast cells","author":"Warmt, Enrico and Grosser, Steffen and Blauth, Eliane and Xie, Xiaofan and Kubitschke, Hans and Stange, Roland and Sauer, Frank and Schnau{\\ss}, J{\\\"o}rg and Tomm, Janina M and von Bergen, Martin and others","journal":"New Journal of Physics","volume":"23","number":"10","pages":"103020","year":"2021","publisher":"IOP Publishing"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"WinickNg2021","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"aa6a9916281e09b1","fields":{"title":"Cell-type specialization is encoded by specific chromatin topologies","author":"Winick-Ng, Warren and Kukalev, Alexander and Harabula, Izabela and Zea-Redondo, Luna and Szab{\\'o}, Dominik and Meijer, Mandy and Serebreni, Leonid and Zhang, Yingnan and Bianco, Simona and Chiariello, Andrea M and others","journal":"Nature","volume":"599","number":"7886","pages":"684--691","year":"2021","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group UK London"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Wintner2020","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"c7ebf9b065bfd2c4","fields":{"title":"A unified linear viscoelastic model of the cell nucleus defines the mechanical contributions of lamins and chromatin","author":"Wintner, Oren and Hirsch-Attas, Nivi and Schlossberg, Miriam and Brofman, Fani and Friedman, Roy and Kupervaser, Meital and Kitsberg, Danny and Buxboim, Amnon","journal":"Advanced Science","volume":"7","number":"8","pages":"1901222","year":"2020","publisher":"Wiley Online Library"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Wolf2013","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"1c4aa6f393a53930","fields":{"title":"Physical limits of cell migration: control by ECM space and nuclear deformation and tuning by proteolysis and traction force","author":"Wolf, Katarina and Te Lindert, Mariska and Krause, Marina and Alexander, Stephanie and Te Riet, Joost and Willis, Amanda L and Hoffman, Robert M and Figdor, Carl G and Weiss, Stephen J and Friedl, Peter","journal":"Journal of Cell Biology","volume":"201","number":"7","pages":"1069--1084","year":"2013","publisher":"The Rockefeller University Press"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Xu2010","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"06f5d3faefa811e3","fields":{"author":"Xu, G. and Knutsen, A. K. and Dikranian, K. Kroenke, C. D. and Bayly, P. V. and Taber L. A.","title":"Axons pull on the brain, but tension does not drive cortical folding.","volume":"132","number":"7","pages":"071013","year":"2010","journal":"J. Biomech. Eng."},"diffs":[{"field":"authors","in_document":"Xu, G. and Knutsen, A. K. and Dikranian, K. Kroenke, C. D. and Bayly, P. V. and Taber L. A.","in_source":"Gang Xu and Andrew K. Knutsen and Krikor Dikranian and Christopher D. Kroenke and Philip V. Bayly and Larry A. Taber","severity":"high"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Yan2019","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"9fdd62bbdfb55f1b","fields":{"title":"Multicellular rosettes drive fluid-solid transition in epithelial tissues","author":"Yan, Le and Bi, Dapeng","journal":"Physical Review X","volume":"9","number":"1","pages":"011029","year":"2019","publisher":"APS"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Yang2009","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"abstract":"A 3D cell-based finite element (FE) model is used to show that the tensions postulated to act along cell-cell interfaces can be determined from tests in which an aggregate of cells is compressed between parallel plates. The aspect ratios $\\kappa$ of individual cells are found to depend on whether they are in contact with the plates, on the surface of the aggregate or in the interior of the mass, a result confirmed experimentally. When the platen spacing is then held constant, interior cells anneal at a rate that depends only on the cell-cell interfacial tension $\\gamma$cc, whereas the annealing rate of plate and surface cells depends on $\\gamma$cc, the cell-plate interfacial tension $\\gamma$cp and the surface tension $\\gamma$cm acting along the cell-medium boundary. The model shows that the ratio of the interfacial tension $\\gamma$cc to the cytoplasmic viscosity $\\mu$ can be determined from the shape history of the interior cells. Cell shape anisotropies make the relationship between cross-sectional shapes and 3D shapes nontrivial. Experiments by others are then used to determine, for the first time, and without compression force data, the interfacial tensions that act in aggregated cells. This property has implications for theories about the forces that drive tissue self-organization. {\\textcopyright} 2009 Biomedical Engineering Society.","author":"Yang, Justina and Brodland, G. Wayne","doi":"10.1007/s10439-009-9649-6","issn":"00906964","journal":"Ann. Biomed. Eng.","keywords":"Aggregate compression,Cell mechanics,Cell shape,Computational modeling,Interfacial tension","title":"{Estimating interfacial tension from the shape histories of cells in compressed aggregates: A computational study}","year":"2009"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Yevick2019","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"557c3f1c5ae2bf8b","fields":{"title":"Structural Redundancy in Supracellular Actomyosin Networks Enables Robust Tissue Folding","journal":"Developmental Cell","volume":"50","number":"5","pages":"586-598.e3","year":"2019","author":"Hannah G. Yevick and Pearson W. Miller and Jörn Dunkel and Adam C. Martin"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Yokouchi2016","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"abstract":"In multicellular organisms, cells adopt various shapes, from flattened sheets of endothelium to dendritic neurons, that allow the cells to function effectively. Here, we elucidated the unique shape of cells in the cornified stratified epithelia of the mammalian epidermis that allows them to achieve homeostasis of the tight junction (TJ) barrier. Using intimate in vivo 3D imaging, we found that the basic shape of TJ-bearing cells is a flattened Kelvin's tetrakaidecahedron (f-TKD), an optimal shape for filling space. In vivo live imaging further elucidated the dynamic replacement of TJs on the edges of f-TKD cells that enables the TJ-bearing cells to translocate across the TJ barrier. We propose a spatiotemporal orchestration model of f-TKD cell turnover, where in the classic context of'form follows function', cell shape provides a fundamental basis for the barrier homeostasis and physical strength of cornified stratified epithelia.","author":"Yokouchi, Mariko and Atsugi, Toru and {Van Logtestijn}, Mark and Tanaka, Reiko J. and Kajimura, Mayumi and Suematsu, Makoto and Furuse, Mikio and Amagai, Masayuki and Kubo, Akiharu","doi":"10.7554/eLife.19593","issn":"2050084X","journal":"Elife","pmid":"27894419","title":"{Epidermal cell turnover across tight junctions based on Kelvin's tetrakaidecahedron cell shape}","year":"2016"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Zhang2020","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"6342cef189f46c7f","fields":{"title":"SARS-CoV-2 infects human neural progenitor cells and brain organoids","author":"Zhang, Bao-Zhong and Chu, Hin and Han, Shuo and Shuai, Huiping and Deng, Jian and Hu, Ye-fan and Gong, Hua-rui and Lee, Andrew Chak-Yiu and Zou, Zijiao and Yau, Thomas and others","journal":"Cell Research","volume":"30","number":"10","pages":"928--931","year":"2020","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Zhang2022","verdict":"verified","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"62c3bd954fb2daf8","fields":{"title":"Topologically-protected interior for three-dimensional confluent cellular collectives","author":"Zhang, Tao and Schwarz, J. M.","journal":"Phys. Rev. Res.","volume":"4","issue":"4","pages":"043148","numpages":"10","year":"2022","month":"Nov","publisher":"American Physical Society","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevResearch.4.043148","url":"https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevResearch.4.043148"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"alipour12","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:35:54Z","response_hash":"fcd29f6db023b946","fields":{"author":"Alipour, E. and Marko, J. F.","title":"Self-organization of domain structures by {D}{N}{A}-l